window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("adoption", {"topics":[{"key":"adoption/change-management","sources":["Kotter, 1996 — Leading Change","Hiatt, 2006 — ADKAR: A Model for Change in Business, Government and Our Community","Rogers, 1962 — Diffusion of Innovations"],"why":"Shipping is not adoption. A typical pattern after an internal AI launch is a burst of curious usage in\nthe first week, then a slow decay back to the old process, while the dashboard still shows the system\n\"live\". Nothing in the code is wrong; the people around it never changed what they do.\n\nAI systems make this harder than ordinary software for three reasons. First, the output is probabilistic,\nso users have to learn *when* to trust it. Users who under-trust redo the work by hand and now spend more\ntime than before, because they read the draft *and* do the task. Users who over-trust let errors through\n(automation bias), and the first public mistake poisons the tool for everyone. Second, AI arrives with an\nimplied question about jobs. If nobody answers it, people answer it themselves, and quiet resistance is a\nrational response. Third, the value usually requires redesigning the work around the tool: if agents are\nstill measured on the old metric, still follow the old checklist and still route through the old\napproval step, the AI step is pure overhead.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in predictable ways. \"Announce it, record a training video, it's intuitive\"\ncreates awareness but not desire, and leaves incentives untouched. \"Mandate it\" produces compliance\ntheatre: people open the tool so the login count looks good and then ignore the output. Change management\nreplaces both with a plan that names the behavior change, finds what blocks it for each group, and\nremoves those blocks one by one.","how":"### Use frameworks as diagnostic checklists\nTwo well-known models cover different levels. **ADKAR** (Prosci) describes the individual: *Awareness* of\nwhy the change is happening, *Desire* to take part, *Knowledge* of how, *Ability* to do it in real work,\nand *Reinforcement* that makes it stick. Its value is diagnostic: find the first stage that is blocked\nfor a group and work there, *because* training (Knowledge) cannot fix a Desire problem, and enthusiasm\n(Desire) cannot fix a missing integration (Ability). **Kotter's eight steps** describe the organization:\ncreate urgency, build a guiding coalition, form a vision, communicate it, remove obstacles, generate\nshort-term wins, consolidate gains, and anchor the new way in culture. The older\nunfreeze-change-refreeze framing, traditionally attributed to Kurt Lewin (historians note the tidy\nthree-step version was largely popularized by later writers), makes the same point compactly: the old\nhabit has to be loosened before the new one can form, and then deliberately locked in. None of these is\nan algorithm; use them to ask \"what is missing for this group?\", not as a sequence of ceremonies.\n\n### 1. Define the change as behavior\n\"Adopt the AI assistant\" cannot be managed or measured. \"Claims adjusters start every file over 30 pages\nfrom the AI summary and verify its cited sections, instead of reading the file front to back\" can. Write\none such sentence per affected role, *because* every later step (training, metrics, incentives) hangs off\nit.\n\n### 2. Assess impact per group\nFor each group, list what changes: tasks, skills, metrics, tools, approvals, and any effect on headcount\nor roles. Be explicit about job impact, *because* silence is filled with rumor. Groups differ: the end\nusers, their team leads, QA reviewers, IT support and compliance each experience a different change.\n\nTreat resistance as data about which stage is blocked, not as an attitude problem. \"It's slower\" is an\nAbility or incentive problem; \"it got the prior-injury note wrong\" is a trust problem; \"why would I train\nmy replacement?\" is a Desire problem rooted in job security; a senior expert's \"I don't need it\" is often\nabout losing the status of being the person who knows. Each needs a different fix, which is why the\ndiagnosis has to be per group.\n\nThe incentive question can usually be made arithmetic. A user adopts the new path when, on the metric\nthey are judged by, it beats the old one *including* the cost of verifying the output:\n`t_ai = t_review_output + t_verify + t_fix_errors` versus `t_manual`. If verification is slow or the\ntask is short, `t_ai > t_manual` and non-adoption is the rational choice, not a training gap.\n\n### 3. Build sponsorship and a change network\nAn executive sponsor who visibly uses the tool and explains the why creates legitimacy. Champions inside\neach team, usually the early adopters in Rogers' diffusion model, create proof, *because* people copy\nrespected peers more readily than memos. Team leads matter most: if a lead says \"don't trust that thing\",\nno amount of central communication wins.\n\n### 4. Change the system around the tool\nUpdate the SOPs, QA checklists and KPIs so the new way is the measured way. Decide when the old path is\nretired and what the fallback is when the AI is down or wrong. *Because* people follow incentives, an\nunchanged scorecard quietly pulls them back to the old process.\n\n### 5. Roll out in phases, and plan for the dip\nStart with a pilot cohort, fix what they hit, then expand. Expect performance to *fall* before it rises\n(often called the J-curve): people are learning a new habit, running old and new paths side by side, and\nhitting first-version bugs. Tell the sponsor in advance, temporarily protect the pilot cohort's\nproductivity targets, and judge the change on the trend after the learning period rather than on week\ntwo, *because* a dip that nobody predicted reads as failure and gets the project cancelled at its\nlowest point.\n\nClose the feedback loop. Publish a \"you said, we did\" list, *because*\nvisible responsiveness is the cheapest way to build desire. Show the measured error rate honestly and\nteach how to verify (citations, confidence cues), which calibrates trust instead of inflating it.\n\n### 6. Reinforce and measure\nTrack leading indicators (weekly active users in the target group, share of eligible tasks started with\nthe AI, override and edit rates) and lagging outcomes (cycle time, quality, cost per case). Usage without\noutcome improvement usually means people are using it but the workflow around it has not changed (or the\ntool is not adding value); outcome improvement without usage means something other than the tool is\ndriving the outcome, so crediting the AI would be wrong.","example":"A hypothetical insurer rolls out an AI claims-file summarizer to 120 adjusters. Usage starts at about 70%\nof adjusters in week one and decays to about 25% by week six. The model's offline eval looks fine, so the\nteam interviews ten adjusters and checks the logs instead of retraining.\n\nThe ADKAR diagnosis differs by group. Adjusters know how to use the tool (Knowledge is fine), but Desire is\nlow: they are scored on claims closed per day, and on short files reading the summary plus checking it\ntakes longer than reading the file. Two team leads tell their teams not to rely on it after a story spread\nabout a summary that missed a prior-injury note. Ability is also weak: the summary opens in a separate tab,\nso verification means switching windows.\n\nThe incentive problem is worth doing as arithmetic (illustrative figures from the interviews): reading a\nfile by hand takes about 0.6 minutes per page; the AI path costs about 10 minutes of fixed overhead\n(read the summary, open and check each cited section) plus about 0.25 minutes per page for the spot\nreading that remains. Break-even is where 0.6p = 10 + 0.25p, so p ≈ 29 pages. On a 12-page file the AI\npath costs 13 minutes against 7 by hand; on an 80-page file it costs 30 against 48. Adjusters who\nabandoned the tool were, on average, handling shorter files: they were right.\n\nInterventions follow the diagnosis. The tool is enabled only for files over 30 pages, where it saves time.\nThe missed-note bug is reproduced, fixed and announced, and the team publishes its golden-set result\n(say, a material omission in 1 of 25 long files) alongside the instruction to check cited sections for\nmedical history. The two skeptical leads join the pilot review and become champions. The QA checklist is\nupdated to accept \"summary verified\" as a valid review step. Citations are moved inline, which cuts the fixed verification overhead and lowers the break-even\nfurther. Usage on eligible files recovers and stays, and cycle time on long files becomes the metric\nreported to the sponsor, rather than the share of all adjusters using the tool, which would have\npressured people to use it on short files where it costs time.","tradeoffs":["A phased pilot surfaces workflow problems cheaply, but it delays org-wide value; skip it only when the change is small and reversible.","Retiring the old path forces adoption and ends dual running, but removes the fallback; keep it while the AI's error rate is still being established.","Heavy executive sponsorship speeds legitimacy, but a sponsor seen as imposing cuts can turn the change into a headcount fight; it flips back to an asset once the sponsor has stated plainly what happens to roles.","Mandating usage gets the numbers up quickly, but produces compliance theatre unless incentives and workflow change first.","Investing in a champion network costs people's time, which pays off at scale and is overkill for a ten-person team."],"pitfalls":["Treating training as the whole change plan; the symptom is high course completion with usage decaying after launch week.","Measuring logins instead of the target behavior; dashboards look healthy while cycle time and quality do not move.","Leaving old KPIs in place, which shows up as users reverting to the manual path whenever they are busy.","Ignoring team leads and middle managers; usage varies sharply team by team with no technical explanation.","Overselling accuracy at launch, so the first visible error triggers a trust collapse and a spike in overrides.","Setting an adoption target of \"everyone uses it for everything\"; the symptom is usage on tasks where the tool is slower, flat or worse outcomes, and resentment.","Reviewing results during the learning dip, which shows up as a sponsor wanting to cancel in week three when the trend was about to turn.","Staying silent on job impact, revealed by rumors, union or works-council questions, and slow passive resistance."],"when":"Plan change management whenever an AI system changes how a group of people does its daily work, which is\nalmost every enterprise deployment beyond a developer tool. The size of the effort scales with the number\nof people affected, how much their metrics and roles change, and how costly mistakes are. Start it during\nscoping, not after launch: the behavior statement and impact assessment belong next to the success\nmetrics. Measure the target behavior and the business outcome, not logins: share of *eligible* tasks done\nthe new way, override and edit rates, time per task on the new path versus the old, and usage segmented\nby team (team-by-team variance with no technical cause points at leads or local incentives). If you\ncannot write the behavior sentence or show the new path beats the old on the user's own metric, fix that\nbefore planning communications. In interviews, the signal is\nwhether you treat low adoption as a diagnosis problem (which ADKAR stage is blocked, for whom) rather than\nas a call for more training or a better model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments the product so the target behavior is measurable, and builds the trust affordances (citations, confidence cues, easy override) users need.","architect":"Owns the design of the human workflow around the system: where review sits, what the fallback is, and which metrics define success.","fde":"Sits with users, diagnoses which stage of change is blocked for which team, and brings concrete workflow and incentive fixes to the sponsor."},"check":{"q":"Six weeks after launch, usage of your AI drafting tool has halved, but offline evals are unchanged. The sponsor wants to fund a model upgrade. What do you do first?","a":"I would not assume the model is the cause, because evals are flat and decay after a launch spike is the classic adoption pattern. I would segment usage by team and task type, interview a handful of users and team leads, and map what I find onto ADKAR to find the first blocked stage. Typical causes are incentives (old KPIs), workflow friction (verification is slow), a trust event (one bad output went viral) or a lead discouraging use. Each has a cheaper fix than a model upgrade. I would come back to the sponsor with the diagnosis, the targeted fixes and the metric that will show whether they worked."},"quiz":[{"q":"In ADKAR terms, what is the main risk of responding to low adoption with more training sessions?","options":["Training sessions are too expensive to run for most teams","Training addresses Knowledge, which may not be the blocked stage if users lack Desire or Ability","Training reduces Awareness by overwhelming users with detail","Training should only happen after the Reinforcement stage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ADKAR is diagnostic: work on the first stage that is blocked. If users understand the tool but are scored on a metric it hurts, or cannot verify outputs easily, more training changes nothing."},{"q":"Which indicator best shows that the intended behavior change has happened?","options":["Number of logins per week across the company","Course completion rate for the launch training","Share of eligible tasks that are started with the AI output in the target group","Number of positive comments in the launch announcement channel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logins and completions measure exposure, not behavior. The share of eligible tasks actually done the new way maps directly to the behavior statement, and can be paired with outcome metrics."},{"q":"Why do AI tools need explicit trust calibration as part of change management, more than ordinary software?","options":["Because AI tools are always less accurate than the manual process they replace","Because regulators require users to sign off on every AI output","Because probabilistic outputs make users either redo the work or let errors through unless they learn when to rely on it","Because AI tools cannot show any evidence for their outputs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deterministic software is right or broken; AI output is right most of the time. Under-trust doubles the work and over-trust lets errors pass, so users need honest error rates and a way to verify."}]},{"key":"adoption/user-training","sources":["Kirkpatrick, 1994 — Evaluating Training Programs: The Four Levels"],"why":"Traditional software training teaches where the buttons are, because the software behaves the same way\nevery time and correctness is the vendor's job. An AI assistant breaks that contract. Two users who click\nthe same button can get different results, the same input can produce a strong answer one day and a\nweaker one after a model update, and correctness is now partly the user's job, because the user is\noften the last check before an output reaches a customer or a record.\n\nThat changes what users need to know. They need to recognize the task types where the system is reliable\nand where it is not; they need a fast way to verify an answer (open the cited source, check the number\nagainst the record); they need to know what not to paste in (customer PII into an unapproved tool, for\nexample); and they need to know how to flag a bad output so it reaches the team that can fix it.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail. A recorded feature demo creates awareness and nothing else, and much of it is\nforgotten quickly if it is not practised soon. \"It's a chat box, it's intuitive\" leaves each user to discover the\nfailure modes alone, usually by being burned in front of a customer. One big launch session covers too\nmuch, before users have the context to absorb it, and is never repeated when the system changes.","how":"### 1. Derive the curriculum from the behavior change\nStart from the target behavior defined in the change plan (\"agents start each reply from the AI draft and\ncheck every policy claim against the linked article\") and work backwards to what a user must know and be\nable to do. Anything that does not support that behavior is optional, *because* attention is the scarce\nresource in training.\n\n### 2. Teach the mental model and the failure modes\nExplain, in the user's terms, what the system does: \"it reads the five most relevant help articles and\ndrafts a reply from them; if the answer is not in those articles, it may guess.\" That one sentence predicts\nthe main failure (confident answers on topics not in the knowledge base) and tells users what to check.\nShow real failures from your evaluation set, *because* people calibrate trust from examples far better\nthan from a stated accuracy number.\n\n### 3. Practise on real tasks, with seeded errors\nHands-on exercises use the users' own kinds of cases. A verification drill gives each person a set of AI\noutputs, some of which contain realistic seeded errors, and asks them to accept, edit or reject each. It\nbuilds the skill that matters most and produces a measurable score. Role-specific tracks matter here:\nthe reviewer, the frontline user and the team lead need different drills.\n\nScore the drill on *two* numbers, not one, *because* there are two ways to be miscalibrated:\n- **Catch rate** = seeded errors caught ÷ seeded errors. Low catch rate means over-trust.\n- **False-reject rate** = correct outputs rejected or rewritten ÷ correct outputs. High false-reject rate\n  means under-trust: the user is doing the task twice and will eventually decide the tool is useless.\n\nA user who rejects everything scores 100% on catch rate, so catch rate alone rewards the wrong behavior.\nThe goal is high catch with low false-reject, plus a verification time per item that fits in the real\nworkflow.\n\nSeed realistic errors, not obvious ones. An invented refund window that looks like a real policy teaches\nsomething; a garbled sentence does not, because the model rarely fails that way. Draw the error types\nfrom the eval set's actual failure modes. Be aware of the base rate, too: a drill might be 25% wrong to\ngive enough practice, but production may be wrong a few times in a hundred, and people checking rarely\nwrong output stop looking (vigilance falls when errors are rare). So teach verification as a fixed,\ncheap habit attached to specific claim types (\"always check numbers, dates and policy terms against the\ncitation\") rather than as a judgment about whether this draft \"feels off\".\n\n### 4. Cover data handling and escalation explicitly\nState what data may and may not go into the tool, why, and what to do on a bad output: the feedback\nbutton, what to write, and when to escalate to a human expert. *Because* these rules carry compliance\nrisk, they deserve a dedicated, short, testable segment rather than a slide buried in the middle.\n\n### 5. Deliver in the workflow, then reinforce\nShort sessions close to go-live, followed by job aids inside the tool (tooltips, a one-page \"when to\ntrust, when to check\" card), office hours, and champions in each team. Spaced reinforcement beats one long\nsession, *because* skills not used soon after training fade quickly (the spacing effect is one of the\nmore robust findings in learning research). Training also cannot fix a slow check: if verifying a claim\ntakes three clicks and a second window, people skip it under time pressure, so the cheapest training\nimprovement is often an inline citation the engineer adds. Train-the-trainer lets the customer's\nown staff carry the program after handoff.\n\n### 6. Measure with the four levels, and retrain on change\nKirkpatrick's four levels give a useful ladder: *Reaction* (did they find it useful), *Learning* (can they\ndo the drill), *Behavior* (do usage logs show the target behavior), *Results* (did the business metric\nmove). Organizations measure levels 1 and 2 far more often than levels 3 and 4, yet 3 and 4 are\nthe ones that show whether the program worked. When the model, prompt or\nknowledge base changes enough to shift behavior, update the failure-mode examples and tell users what is\ndifferent, *because* their calibrated trust is now calibrated to an old system.","example":"A hypothetical support team of 60 agents is getting an AI reply drafter grounded in the help center. The\neval shows drafts are usually accurate on documented topics but sometimes invent policy details on\nundocumented ones.\n\nTraining is three 45-minute sessions in the go-live week, by team. Session one explains the mental model\n(\"it only knows the help center; if the answer is not there it may guess\") and shows four real eval\nfailures. Session two is a verification drill: 20 drafts, 5 with seeded errors (an invented refund window,\na wrong plan name, a missing caveat). Before coaching, agents catch on average 2 of the 5; after a\nwalk-through of \"click the citation, check every number and policy claim\", a second drill set gets an\naverage of 4 of 5. The drill also tracks the other error: before coaching, agents rewrote on average 4\nof the 15 correct drafts \"to be safe\", which is the under-trust that makes the tool feel slower than\ntyping; after coaching, with an explicit rule for what to check and what to leave alone, that falls to\n1 of 15. Both numbers are reported, because improving catch rate by making people rewrite everything\nwould be a regression. Session three covers what customer data may be pasted into free-text fields, the\nfeedback button, and when to hand off to a tier-2 specialist.\n\nReinforcement follows: a pinned checklist in the drafting panel, two champions per team, and office hours\ntwice a week for a month. At level 3, logs show the share of replies sent with zero edits on undocumented\ntopics falling, which is the desired behavior, while overall draft use rises. At level 4 the team reports\nhandle time and the QA error rate to the sponsor. When the knowledge base is restructured two months later,\na 15-minute refresher shows what changed. (All figures are illustrative.)","tradeoffs":["Live, hands-on sessions build real verification skill, but cost user hours; recorded material scales better when the tool is low-risk.","Role-specific tracks fit each job closely, but multiply preparation work; one shared track is fine when roles use the tool identically.","Showing real failures calibrates trust, but can scare off adopters if presented without the verification method that handles them.","Training close to go-live improves retention, but compresses the schedule; train too early and people forget before they can practise.","Customer-run train-the-trainer scales and survives handoff, but quality drifts unless you supply materials and check drill scores."],"pitfalls":["Teaching features instead of failure modes; the symptom is users who know every button yet forward wrong answers unchecked.","Measuring only satisfaction surveys, which look positive while usage logs show the target behavior never appeared.","Never retraining after model or prompt changes, revealed by a sudden rise in overrides or complaints after an update.","Treating data-handling rules as an aside, discovered later when sensitive data shows up in prompts or logs.","Training once at launch with no reinforcement, shown by usage and drill scores sliding over the following weeks.","Seeding drills with obvious errors, revealed by near-perfect drill scores alongside QA still finding unchecked policy mistakes in production.","Scoring only the catch rate, which shows up as heavy editing of correct drafts and handle time rising instead of falling."],"when":"Run structured training whenever users are expected to rely on AI output in work that matters, and\nespecially when they are the last check before the output reaches a customer, a record or a decision.\nScale the effort with the cost of errors and the number of users: a low-risk internal summarizer may need\na job aid and a short video; a drafting tool used for regulated customer communication needs drills,\ndata-handling rules and measured verification skill. Measure at behavior and results level, not just\nreaction: drill catch rate and false-reject rate (learning), citation-open rate and edit rate on\nhigh-risk claim types in the logs (behavior), and QA error rate and handle time (results). If the\nbehavior metrics do not move after training, check the UI cost of verifying before adding more\nsessions. In interviews, a strong answer explains why AI training must teach verification and failure\nmodes, and how you would tell whether it worked.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the in-product affordances training relies on: inline citations, feedback buttons, tooltips and logging that shows whether verification happens.","architect":"Decides where human review sits and which outputs require it, which in turn sets what each role must be trained to check.","fde":"Runs the sessions with the customer's real cases, builds the seeded-error drills, and trains internal champions who own training after handoff."},"check":{"q":"Training satisfaction scores are high, yet QA finds agents are sending AI drafts with policy errors. What is wrong with the program, and how would you change it?","a":"The program is probably measuring reaction, not learning or behavior, and teaching what the tool does rather than how it fails. I would add a verification drill with seeded, realistic policy errors to measure whether agents can catch them, teach a concrete checking method (open the citation, check every policy claim and number), and show real failures from the eval set. Then I would track behavior in logs, such as edit rates on undocumented topics, and the QA error rate as the result. I would watch the false-reject rate too, so the fix does not swing\nagents into rewriting every draft. I would also check whether the UI makes verification fast, because a slow check gets skipped regardless of training."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which Kirkpatrick level is shown by usage logs where agents now open citations before sending drafts?","options":["Reaction","Learning","Behavior","Results"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Behavior is whether people apply the skill on the job, which logs can show. Learning would be a drill score in training; Results would be the change in the business metric such as QA errors."},{"q":"Why are seeded-error drills more useful than a quiz on product features for AI tools?","options":["Feature quizzes are harder to grade automatically","They train and measure the skill users most need: catching plausible but wrong outputs in realistic cases","They prove the model is accurate enough to deploy","They remove the need for human review in production"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Users are often the last check, so recognizing wrong outputs is the critical skill. A drill measures it directly; it says nothing about model accuracy and does not replace review."},{"q":"After a major model upgrade, what should happen to user training?","options":["Nothing, as long as the interface has not changed","Users should be retrained from scratch with the full original program","Update the failure-mode examples and give a short refresher on what behaves differently","Training should pause until the new model's accuracy reaches 100%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Users' trust is calibrated to the old system's behavior. A targeted refresher on what changed recalibrates it cheaply; a full retrain wastes time and waiting for perfection never ends."}]},{"key":"adoption/workshops","sources":["Gray, Brown & Macanufo, 2010 — Gamestorming","Lipmanowicz & McCandless, 2013 — The Surprising Power of Liberating Structures"],"why":"AI projects depend on knowledge that lives in people's heads: which cases are hard, what \"good\" looks like\nfor an answer, where the workflow really breaks, which data is allowed. That knowledge is spread across\nsubject-matter experts, operators, IT, security and a sponsor who rarely sit together. Collecting it one\ninterview at a time is slow and produces contradictions you discover only when the system ships. Asking\nfor it by email produces short answers from the busiest people and silence from the rest.\n\nAI adds a sharper version of the problem. Quality criteria for generative output are often tacit: two\nexperts can look at the same drafted answer and disagree on whether it is acceptable, and neither has\nwritten the rule down. You cannot build an eval, a prompt or a training program until that disagreement is\nsurfaced and resolved, and the fastest way to do that is to put the experts in front of the same real\nexamples at the same time.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail differently. An open \"brainstorm\" with no structure gets dominated by the most\nsenior or loudest voice and ends with a list of ideas nobody owns. A presentation dressed up as a workshop\n(you talk, they listen) captures nothing. A well-run workshop is designed backwards from the artifact it\nmust produce, with structure that makes everyone's knowledge visible before it is debated.","how":"### 1. Fix the output and the decision first\nWrite down what will exist at the end: \"a rubric with 4 to 6 criteria that 5 claims experts agree on\" or\n\"a top-3 use-case shortlist with an owner and data source each\". Everything else is designed backwards\nfrom this, *because* a workshop without a defined artifact drifts into discussion.\n\nThen budget the agenda from the artifact, not from the calendar slot. If the output needs 20 examples\nlabelled and every disagreement discussed, estimate how many will split and how long each discussion\ntakes; a three-hour slot that needs four hours of discussion produces a half-finished rubric and a\n\"we'll continue by email\" that never converges.\n\n### 2. Choose participants for knowledge and authority\nInvite the people who do the work, the people who can decide, and anyone with a veto (security, data\nowner, legal) for the parts that concern them. Keep the working group small, often under about ten,\n*because* participation per person falls fast as groups grow. Separate sessions are better than one huge\none when audiences need different things.\n\n### 3. Prepare real material and pre-work\nBring the customer's actual documents, tickets or outputs, anonymized as needed, and send short pre-work\n(list your three most painful cases). Real material grounds the conversation in facts *because* people\nargue less about a specific ticket than about abstractions.\n\n### 4. Structure: diverge, then converge\nOpen with silent individual work (sticky notes, labeling, writing) before any discussion, *because* it\nstops the first senior opinion from anchoring everyone else. Then share in small groups and in plenary,\nfor example with the 1-2-4-All pattern (alone, pairs, fours, whole group), cluster, and converge with\nexplicit criteria: dot voting, an impact-versus-feasibility grid, or discussing only the disagreements.\nKeep a parking lot for off-topic items so they are captured without derailing the session.\n\nAgree the decision rule before the decision, *because* a group that has not agreed how to decide will\nargue about the process as soon as the outcome is close. Options: the accountable owner decides after\nhearing everyone; consent (adopt unless someone has a reasoned objection); or a vote with the owner\nbreaking ties. Hard constraints (legal, regulatory, security) are not voted on; the person who owns them\nrules, and the group designs around the ruling.\n\n### Measure agreement, not just whether people nodded\nFor labelling and rubric sessions, compute agreement on the silent first pass and again after the\nrubric. Raw percent agreement is easy to read but flatters you when one label dominates: if 90% of\noutputs are acceptable, two raters who say \"accept\" to everything agree 90% of the time while\ndisagreeing on every case that matters. Chance-corrected statistics (Cohen's kappa for two raters,\nFleiss' kappa for more) correct for this. In the room, a simpler readout works: the count of items where\neveryone agrees, and the list of split items, which is the agenda for the discussion. If agreement does\nnot rise after the rubric is written, the criteria are still ambiguous or the experts genuinely want\ndifferent things, and that has to be escalated to whoever owns the definition of \"good\".\n\n### 5. Facilitate actively\nThe facilitator owns time, makes quiet participants heard, and stops solution debates that belong\nelsewhere. On a customer engagement the FDE is often both facilitator and domain outsider, which is useful:\nnaive questions expose tacit rules.\n\n### 6. Capture live and follow up fast\nWrite outputs into the artifact during the session, not from memory later. Send a summary within a day or\ntwo with decisions, open questions, owners and dates, *because* agreement decays quickly and the summary\nbecomes the record people refer to.\n\n### Common AI workshop types\nUse-case discovery (rank candidates by value and feasibility), workflow mapping (as-is and to-be, where AI\nsits and who reviews), rubric and labeling sessions (agree on what good output means), and enablement or\nprompting sessions for users. Each has a different artifact, so do not merge them.","example":"A hypothetical insurer needs acceptance criteria for AI-drafted claim-denial letters. The team runs a\nthree-hour rubric workshop with five senior claims handlers, a compliance officer and the FDE as\nfacilitator.\n\nThe agenda is designed backwards from that output (times illustrative): 15 minutes framing and the\ndecision rule (compliance rules on legal requirements, the claims director's delegate on everything\nelse); 30 minutes silent labeling; 15 minutes revealing the label grid; about 60 minutes on the split\ncases at roughly 5 minutes each; 30 minutes drafting criteria; 20 minutes relabeling; 10 minutes on\nowners and open questions. That budget only works if about 10 to 12 cases split, which is why the\nfacilitator picks the 20 drafts to include obvious passes and obvious fails as well as hard cases.\n\nPre-work: each handler sends two letters they consider excellent and two they would reject. In the session,\neveryone first labels the same 20 AI drafts silently as accept, edit or reject. On the first pass the five\nhandlers agree unanimously on only 9 of the 20. Rather than debate all 20, the group discusses just the 11\nsplit cases. The disagreements cluster into four themes: whether the letter cites the exact policy clause,\nwhether the tone is acceptable for a bereaved claimant, whether appeal rights are stated, and whether the\nletter speculates about facts not in the file. Compliance rules that appeal rights are mandatory, which\nturns one theme from preference into a hard requirement.\n\nThe group writes four criteria with a pass and a fail example each, then relabels 10 fresh drafts with the\nrubric; unanimous agreement rises to 8 of 10. Output: a written rubric, 30 labeled examples for the golden\nset, two open questions with owners, and a follow-up summary sent the next morning. (Figures are\nillustrative.)","tradeoffs":["In-person workshops build trust and energy, but cost travel and calendar time; remote works well for short, focused labeling sessions.","Inviting decision-makers lets the session commit, but their presence can silence junior experts unless silent individual work comes first.","Tight structure produces predictable artifacts, but can miss surprising insights; leave a slot for open discussion when exploring a new domain.","Broad attendance builds buy-in, yet reduces depth per person; split into focused sessions when more than about ten people need to contribute.","A full-day session gets much done at once, but fatigue erodes the afternoon; two half-days often produce better output."],"pitfalls":["No defined output, so the session ends with energy but no artifact; the symptom is a follow-up email that only says \"great session\".","Starting with open discussion, letting the most senior voice anchor everyone; the output mirrors one person's view.","Using hypothetical examples instead of real data, revealed later when the rubric fails on actual cases.","Missing the veto holder, so a security or compliance objection arrives weeks later and invalidates agreed decisions.","Slow follow-up; within a week participants remember different decisions and reopen settled questions.","Running a \"workshop\" to ratify a decision already made, which participants detect quickly; the symptom is polite silence and an output nobody defends later.","No agreed decision rule, revealed when the last hour is spent arguing about who gets to decide rather than about the criteria."],"when":"Reach for a workshop when the knowledge you need is spread across several people, when they are likely to\ndisagree, or when you need a joint commitment rather than individual opinions: discovery, workflow\nredesign, defining quality criteria, labeling a first golden set, and user enablement. Do not use one when a\nsingle expert can answer by message, or when the decision is already made and only needs communicating.\nJudge a workshop by its artifact and by whether decisions held a month later; for labelling sessions,\nalso by whether agreement measurably rose between the first pass and the relabel. In interviews, describe the\noutput you would design for and how you would stop anchoring.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Prepares real examples and tooling for labeling sessions, and turns the agreed rubric and labels into eval cases and prompt changes.","architect":"Uses workflow and discovery workshops to settle boundaries: where AI sits, who reviews, which systems and data are in scope.","fde":"Designs and facilitates the sessions on site, extracts tacit expert rules through naive questions, and owns the follow-up summary."},"check":{"q":"Your client's experts keep disagreeing about whether the AI's answers are \"good enough\". How would you design a workshop to resolve this, and why that structure?","a":"I would design backwards from a written rubric plus a labeled example set. Experts would first label the same real outputs silently, which makes disagreement measurable and stops anchoring on the most senior person. We would then discuss only the cases where labels split, cluster the reasons into criteria, and let the person with authority (often compliance or the process owner) rule on which criteria are mandatory. Finally we would relabel fresh examples with the rubric to check that agreement improved. The output feeds the eval set, and a summary with owners goes out within a day."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why start a workshop segment with silent individual work before group discussion?","options":["It shortens the session by skipping discussion","It captures everyone's view before the first opinion, often the most senior, anchors the group","It lets the facilitator grade participants individually","It is required for remote sessions only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Anchoring and deference to seniority distort group output. Writing or labeling alone first makes every view visible, and the following discussion can focus on genuine disagreements."},{"q":"In a labeling workshop, five experts agree on 9 of 20 outputs. What is the most efficient next step?","options":["Replace the experts with a single senior reviewer","Average the labels and move on","Discuss only the split cases and turn the reasons for disagreement into explicit criteria","Ask the model to label the cases instead"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The split cases contain the tacit rules that differ between experts. Turning them into criteria makes the rubric explicit; averaging or delegating hides the disagreement that will resurface later."},{"q":"What best indicates that a discovery workshop succeeded?","options":["Participants rated it highly in the feedback form","The session ran to the planned agenda exactly","A defined artifact exists with owners, and the decisions are still holding weeks later","Every attendee spoke at least once"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A workshop is a means to an artifact and a durable decision. Satisfaction and agenda adherence can be high in a session that produced nothing anyone acts on."}]},{"key":"adoption/stakeholder-management","sources":[],"why":"An enterprise AI deployment touches more groups than most software projects. The sponsor funds it, a\nprocess owner's team uses it, IT runs the integration, security and legal review the data flow, a data\nowner controls access to the source systems, procurement handles the contract, and in some regions a works\ncouncil or union must be consulted when work changes. Each can slow or stop the project, and each defines\nsuccess differently: the sponsor wants a business metric, the users want less tedium and no blame,\nsecurity wants no new exposure, the data owner wants no surprises.\n\nAI raises the stakes in two ways. First, sending company data to a model triggers reviews (data\nclassification, residency, vendor risk) that ordinary tools may not, and those reviewers have real veto\npower. Second, quality is probabilistic, so expectations need active management: a sponsor who heard\n\"it works\" in the demo will treat a 5% error rate as a failure unless someone set that expectation early.\n\nThe naive approach, talking mostly to the sponsor and the friendly champion, fails because it optimizes\nfor the people already on your side. Blockers are discovered when they block, typically at the security\nreview or the go-live decision, when changes are most expensive. Treating everyone the same (a weekly\nupdate email to all) fails too: it is noise to most readers and too shallow for the few who decide.","how":"### 1. Identify everyone, including the quiet vetoes\nList people and groups, not departments. For each, ask: can they approve, block, fund, or are they\naffected? Probe specifically for the roles AI projects forget: data owners, security, privacy, legal,\ncompliance, IT operations, team leads of end users, and employee representatives. Ask each stakeholder\n\"who else should I talk to?\", *because* informal vetoes are often invisible on the org chart.\n\n### 2. Map influence, interest and stance\nThe power-interest grid (usually attributed to Mendelow) sorts people into four groups: high power and high interest (manage closely),\nhigh power and low interest (keep satisfied), low power and high interest (keep informed), low power and\nlow interest (monitor). Add a third attribute, current stance (supporter, neutral, skeptic, blocker), and\nwhat each person needs to believe to support the project and what they fear. *Because* engagement effort is\nfinite, the grid tells you where to spend it. Stance is the attribute that changes, so record the\nevidence for it (\"asked twice about data residency\") rather than a label, and expect to revise it.\n\nFor each important stakeholder, write down what they are measured on and what this project does to that\nmeasure. A security lead is rewarded for incidents that did not happen and gains nothing from your\nlaunch; a team lead is judged on throughput that the pilot will dent for a few weeks; a data owner is\nblamed if their data leaks through someone else's system. Most \"irrational\" resistance becomes\npredictable once you see the project from their scorecard, and the fix is usually to change what the\nproject costs them (a complete review package, a protected target during the pilot, a narrower data\nscope), not to argue harder about the benefits.\n\n### 3. Define decision rights\nFor each key decision (scope, data access, go-live, rollback), agree who is responsible, accountable,\nconsulted and informed (RACI). This prevents the classic stall where three people each think another one\napproves the release, *because* an AI go-live usually needs sign-off from several functions.\n\n### 4. Sequence by lead time, not by friendliness\nSome approvals have long, fixed lead times: a security or vendor-risk review queue, a data-access\nrequest, a works-council consultation, a procurement cycle. Lay them out like a project plan and work\nbackwards from go-live, *because* the latest-starting long-lead review sets the real launch date\nregardless of how fast the engineering goes. If go-live is in ten weeks and the security queue is\nrunning at six, the review package must be submitted by week four, which means the data-flow design\nmust be stable before then.\n\n### 5. Plan engagement per person\nManage-closely stakeholders get one-to-ones and early drafts. Security and legal get the architecture and\ndata-flow documentation before they ask, *because* a reviewer who receives a complete package early is\nfar less likely to block late. Users and team leads get workshops and pilots. Executives get short\ndecision-focused updates.\n\n### 6. Escalate with options, not complaints\nWhen a stakeholder blocks and one-to-one work has not resolved it, escalate through the sponsor, but\nbring the blocker's concern stated fairly, two or three options (narrow scope, add a control, delay)\nand their costs. Tell the blocker before you escalate. Escalation framed as \"they are in the way\"\ncosts you the relationship for the rest of the project; escalation framed as \"we need a decision\nbetween these options\" usually gets one.\n\n### 7. Maintain the map\nKeep a register with owner, stance, last contact and next action, review it weekly, and update it when\npeople change roles. When a sponsor leaves, the successor inherits a project they did not choose and may cut it, so\nbuild relationships with more than one senior supporter.","example":"A minimal stakeholder register, the kind an FDE keeps alongside the project plan, with a check that flags\nwhere attention is overdue. The rule encodes the grid: high-power people who are not supporters, or whom\nnobody has spoken to recently, need action this week.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nstakeholders = [\n    # name, role, power 1-5, interest 1-5, stance, last contact\n    (\"Priya\", \"VP Ops (sponsor)\",         5, 5, \"supporter\", date(2026, 10, 14)),\n    (\"Marc\",  \"CISO delegate\",            5, 2, \"skeptic\",   date(2026, 9, 2)),\n    (\"Lena\",  \"Data owner, claims DB\",    4, 2, \"neutral\",   date(2026, 9, 20)),\n    (\"Tom\",   \"Team lead, adjusters\",     3, 5, \"blocker\",   date(2026, 10, 1)),\n    (\"Ana\",   \"Adjuster (champion)\",      2, 5, \"supporter\", date(2026, 10, 16)),\n]\n\ndef quadrant(power, interest):\n    if power >= 4:\n        return \"manage closely\" if interest >= 4 else \"keep satisfied\"\n    return \"keep informed\" if interest >= 4 else \"monitor\"\n\ndef actions(today, stale_days=21):\n    for name, role, p, i, stance, last in stakeholders:\n        q, age = quadrant(p, i), (today - last).days\n        flags = []\n        if p >= 4 and stance != \"supporter\":\n            flags.append(f\"high power, {stance}\")\n        if stance == \"blocker\":\n            flags.append(\"blocker: find out what they fear\")\n        if p >= 4 and age > stale_days:\n            flags.append(f\"no contact for {age} days\")\n        if flags:\n            print(f\"{name:5} {role:24} [{q}] -> \" + \"; \".join(flags))\n\nactions(date(2026, 10, 20))\n```\n\nOn this data the check flags Marc (skeptic with a veto, 48 days without contact: send the data-flow\npackage and book a review), Lena (neutral data owner, 30 days: confirm access terms) and Tom (a team lead\nblocking adoption: a one-to-one about what he fears, usually blame for AI errors or his team's metrics).\nThe sponsor and the champion need nothing this week.","tradeoffs":["Mapping everyone formally catches hidden vetoes, but costs time; a small internal tool with one team needs only a short list.","Engaging blockers early surfaces objections while they are cheap, but can slow momentum; skip only when their approval is genuinely not required.","A single strong sponsor makes decisions fast, but creates a single point of failure if that person moves; cultivate a second senior supporter.","Tailoring messages per stakeholder improves relevance, but risks inconsistent stories; keep one source of truth that every message draws from.","Sharing the error rate openly sets realistic expectations, but can alarm cautious stakeholders unless paired with the mitigation and the baseline."],"pitfalls":["Talking mainly to supporters; the symptom is a surprise \"no\" from security or legal at the go-live review.","Leaving decision rights implicit, revealed when go-live waits for weeks because nobody knows who signs off.","Ignoring team leads of end users, which shows up as adoption varying sharply between teams with the same tool.","Letting the sponsor's expectations drift from the eval results, so a normal error rate is read as project failure.","Never updating the map after reorganizations, discovered when a new executive cancels a project they never heard of.","Engaging long-lead reviewers in priority order of friendliness rather than lead time, revealed when the build is done and the project waits weeks in a review queue."],"when":"Keep a stakeholder map on any AI deployment that crosses team boundaries, needs data access from another\nowner, or requires security or compliance review, which covers most enterprise work. Build it in the first\nweek of scoping and review it weekly through rollout and hypercare. Measure it indirectly: no late vetoes,\ndecisions made on schedule, approvals submitted ahead of each reviewer's lead time, and sponsors whose\nexpectations match the evals. For a small internal tool used\nby one team, a short list of approvers is enough. In interviews, show that you look for blockers and veto\nholders first, not just sponsors.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the artifacts reviewers need, such as data-flow diagrams, eval results and access logs, and answers technical objections precisely.","architect":"Owns the decision rights for architecture, data access and security sign-off, and makes sure reviewers see designs before they harden.","fde":"Maintains the map inside the customer, finds informal vetoes by asking who else matters, and converts skeptics through one-to-ones."},"check":{"q":"Two weeks before go-live, the customer's security team, never formally engaged, asks to review the system and signals concern. How do you respond, and what would you have done differently?","a":"Now: treat it as legitimate, not an obstacle. I would meet them immediately with a complete package (architecture and data-flow diagram, data classification, where data is stored and for how long, vendor terms, access controls, logging) and ask for their specific concerns in writing so we can resolve them one by one. I would tell the sponsor honestly that go-live may move. Earlier: security has a veto on any system that sends company data to a model, so they belong in the high-power quadrant from week one, with their review lead time on the plan and the data-flow design reviewed before integration was built. I would also ask what they are accountable for, because a package that answers their specific exposure questions moves faster than one that argues the business value."},"quiz":[{"q":"On a power-interest grid, a security reviewer who rarely engages but can block go-live belongs where?","options":["Monitor, because their interest is low","Keep informed, because they only need status updates","Keep satisfied, because high power means their objection can stop the project","Manage closely, because every reviewer must attend weekly meetings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"High power with low interest means \"keep satisfied\": give them what they need to approve, early and complete. Monitoring them risks a late veto; weekly meetings waste their time."},{"q":"Why define a RACI for the go-live decision on an AI deployment?","options":["To make the sponsor solely responsible for any errors","Because go-live usually needs several functions, and without explicit decision rights the release stalls","Because RACI documents are required by most AI regulations","To replace the need for security and compliance reviews"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AI go-lives typically need sign-off from operations, security, compliance and IT. A RACI makes the accountable approver explicit, so nobody waits for an approval nobody believes is theirs."},{"q":"A team lead is openly discouraging his team from using the tool. What is the best first move?","options":["Escalate to the sponsor to mandate usage on his team","Exclude his team from the rollout","Meet him one-to-one to learn what he fears, such as blame for AI errors or damage to team metrics","Send his team additional training materials"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blockers usually have a concrete concern. Understanding it allows a real fix; mandating through the sponsor tends to produce compliance theatre, and more training does not address his objection."}]},{"key":"adoption/exec-communication","sources":["Minto, 1987 — The Pyramid Principle"],"why":"Executives allocate money, people and political cover across many initiatives at once. They see your\nproject for a few minutes a week, usually between other meetings, and decide from that whether to keep\nfunding it, expand it, or intervene. Their attention, not your work, is the bottleneck.\n\nEngineers default to chronological, bottom-up reporting: what we did, what we found, and finally what it\nmeans. That order mirrors how the work happened, but forces the reader to do the synthesis, and the\nconclusion often arrives after their attention has gone. Worse, technical framing (\"retrieval recall@10\nimproved from 0.71 to 0.83\") leaves the executive unable to tell whether this is good news for the\nbusiness.\n\nAI makes it harder in three ways. Quality is a rate, not a yes or no, so \"it works\" and \"it fails\" are both\nmisleading; executives need the error rate translated into cases, cost and risk. Results are uncertain\nearly, so honest ranges beat false precision. And AI projects carry reputational and compliance risk, so\nbad news that arrives late destroys trust faster than the bad news itself. The naive alternatives,\ndumping the full status report or polishing everything green, both fail: one is not read, the other is\nbelieved until it is not.","how":"### 1. Lead with the answer\nPut the conclusion and any ask in the first two lines: bottom line up front (BLUF). Minto's Pyramid\nPrinciple gives the structure: state the governing point, support it with a few grouped arguments, support\neach with evidence. *Because* the reader may stop at any point, each level should stand on its own.\nSCQA (situation, complication, question, answer) is a useful way to frame the opening when context is\nneeded: what is true, what changed, what question that raises, what you recommend.\n\n### 2. Speak in business outcomes and baselines\nTranslate model metrics into the KPI the executive owns, against the baseline measured before deployment:\n\"average handling time on long claims is down from 42 to 31 minutes\" rather than \"summary ROUGE improved\".\n*Because* the executive's question is always \"is this worth it\", every number should connect to value,\ncost or risk. The translation is usually one line of arithmetic, and showing it makes the number\ncheckable:\n\n```\nvalue per month   = volume × time saved per item × loaded cost per hour\nescaped errors    = volume × error rate × (1 − catch rate at review)\n```\n\nA per-item saving means little to an executive; \"about 220 adjuster-hours a month\" lets them compare it\nwith the cost of the system and with their other options.\n\n### 3. Make uncertainty and error rates concrete\nExpress quality per volume and with consequence: \"about 3 in 100 drafts need a material correction; all\nare caught at human review; zero reached customers this month\". Give ranges when the sample is small,\nand say what would change the estimate. *Because* executives make risk decisions, they need to know the\nsize and containment of the failure, not just the average. \"3% error rate\" sounds small; at 20,000\ndrafts a month it is 600 corrections, and if review catches 95% of them, about 30 a month reach\ncustomers. Whether 30 is acceptable is the executive's decision, and they cannot make it from \"97%\naccurate\". Be explicit about the sample, too: zero failures in 50 cases is still consistent with a true\nfailure rate as high as about 6% (the \"rule of three\" gives roughly 3/n as the upper 95% bound when no\nfailures are seen), so say \"no failures yet in 50 cases\" rather than \"no failures\".\n\n### 4. Ask for a decision explicitly\nWhen you need something, state the decision, the options, your recommendation, the cost of each, and the\ndate by which it must be made. *Because* \"FYI\" items get read and forgotten, an update without a clear ask\ncannot unblock you.\n\n### 5. No surprises: escalate early, pre-wire big messages\nReport risks while they are still yellow, with a mitigation. Before a steering meeting where you will\ndeliver difficult news, brief the sponsor one-to-one, *because* executives react badly to learning\nsomething in front of peers that they could have known earlier.\n\n### 6. Fix the format, the cadence and the status rules\nA one-page update with a stable structure (headline, status with trend, KPI versus baseline and target,\ntop risks, decisions needed) lets the reader compare week to week. Keep the detail in an appendix for\nthose who want it.\n\nDefine what each status colour means at the start, in terms the sponsor agreed: for example, green is\n\"on track for the target date and KPI with no unmitigated risk\", amber is \"at risk, mitigation in\nplace, no decision needed yet\", red is \"will miss without a decision from you\". *Because* an undefined\ncolour is chosen by the author's optimism, undefined status drifts toward green until it jumps to red,\nwhich is how watermelon reporting happens without anyone intending to mislead. Amber with a mitigation\nis a normal, healthy state for a pilot; reporting it is what earns belief in your greens.\n\n### 7. Match the channel to the decision\nA written pre-read works when the reader needs to think before deciding; a live discussion works when\noptions must be negotiated; a two-line message works for a yes/no that is already pre-wired. Send\npre-reads early enough to be read, and open the meeting with the decision, not a recap of the\ndocument.","example":"A hypothetical engineer drafts a steering update for the VP of Operations about an AI claims-summary\npilot:\n\n\"This sprint we migrated to a new embedding model, rebuilt the index, fixed a PDF parsing bug affecting\nscanned files and ran the eval suite. Recall@10 went from 0.71 to 0.83. We are also seeing some issues\nwith handwritten notes. Usage is around 60%. Next sprint we will look at OCR options.\"\n\nThe VP cannot tell whether the pilot is on track, what the numbers mean or what is being asked. The\nrewrite, using the pyramid structure:\n\n\"**Bottom line:** the pilot is on track to hit its target, and I need a decision on OCR budget by 3\nNovember.\n**Value:** handling time on long claims is down from 42 to 31 minutes against a 30-minute target, across\nthe 40 adjusters in the pilot. At about 1,200 long claims a month that is roughly 220 adjuster-hours a\nmonth (1,200 × 11 minutes).\n**Quality:** about 3 in 100 summaries need a material correction; all were caught at adjuster review and\nnone reached a claimant.\n**Risk:** about one file in eight contains handwritten notes, which the system cannot read today, so\nthose files fall back to manual review.\n**Decision:** fund a specialist OCR service (estimated cost in the appendix) to extend coverage to those\nfiles, or accept manual handling for them. I recommend funding it, because it is the largest remaining\ngap to the target.\"\n\nThe technical details move to an appendix. (All figures are illustrative.)","tradeoffs":["Leading with the conclusion saves executive time, but needs the analysis finished first; when it is not, say what you know and when you will know more.","Translating into business metrics makes value visible, but hides technical nuance; keep an appendix for technical reviewers who need it.","Reporting risks early builds trust, but may trigger premature intervention; pair each risk with a mitigation and an owner.","A fixed one-page format enables week-to-week comparison, but can feel rigid when a crisis needs a dedicated note.","Pre-wiring the sponsor avoids public surprises, but adds a meeting; skip it only when the news is routine."],"pitfalls":["Chronological reporting that buries the conclusion; the symptom is executives asking \"so is this on track?\" after every update.","Reporting model metrics without a business translation, revealed when steering discussions never touch value.","Watermelon status (green outside, red inside) that hides problems until they explode, destroying trust in later updates.","Updates without an explicit ask, so blockers wait weeks because nobody realized a decision was needed.","Quoting a single accuracy number without volume, consequence or uncertainty, leading to over-confidence or panic.","Leaving status colours undefined, which shows up as a project reported green for weeks that turns red in a single update.","Claiming \"no errors\" from a small sample, revealed when the first production failures arrive and the executive feels misled."],"when":"Use this structure for any communication with someone who decides about funding, scope or risk but does\nnot do the work: steering updates, escalations, go/no-go recommendations and quarterly value reviews. With\npeers who need detail, lead with the conclusion but include the technical depth. Measure success by\nwhether decisions arrive on time, whether executives can restate the status and the ask after reading\n(ask the sponsor to play it back), and whether their understanding of status matches reality: a red\nthat was never preceded by an amber is a reporting failure, not just a project one. In\ninterviews, you may be asked to explain a technical result to an executive; lead with the outcome and the\nask, give one number tied to value, and state the main risk.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns eval and telemetry output into the few business-facing numbers an update needs, with honest error rates and ranges.","architect":"Frames trade-offs (cost, risk, time) as explicit options with a recommendation so executives can decide quickly.","fde":"Writes the customer's steering updates, pre-wires the sponsor before difficult meetings, and escalates risks while they are still recoverable."},"check":{"q":"Your pilot's accuracy is below the target agreed with the customer's executive, but a fix is likely. How do you communicate this?","a":"Early and directly, before the steering meeting, in a one-to-one with the sponsor. I would lead with the bottom line: we are below target, here is the gap in business terms (what share of cases need correction and whether any reached customers), here is the cause, here is the fix with a realistic date and its uncertainty, and here is the decision I need, such as extending the pilot by three weeks or narrowing scope to the case types already above target. I would not bury it or polish it green, because finding out later costs far more trust than the gap itself."},"quiz":[{"q":"What should come first in an executive status update?","options":["A chronological list of work completed since the last update","The technical metrics with their definitions","The conclusion and any decision you need from the reader","The methodology used to evaluate the system"],"answer":2,"explanation":"BLUF and the Pyramid Principle put the governing point first because executives may stop reading at any time. Chronology and methodology belong lower or in an appendix."},{"q":"Which phrasing best communicates AI quality to an executive?","options":["\"Our F1 score improved by 0.12 this sprint\"","\"The model is highly accurate\"","\"About 3 in 100 drafts need correction; all were caught at review and none reached customers\"","\"The model hallucinates sometimes, as all LLMs do\""],"answer":2,"explanation":"Executives make risk decisions, so they need the rate in volume terms plus whether errors are contained. Raw metrics, vague praise or fatalism give them nothing to decide with."},{"q":"Why brief the sponsor one-to-one before presenting bad news at a steering meeting?","options":["To get permission to leave the bad news out of the meeting","So the sponsor is not surprised in front of peers and can help shape the response","Because steering committees cannot discuss risks formally","To shorten the steering meeting agenda"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pre-wiring keeps trust and turns the sponsor into an ally for the proposed fix. Hiding the news would violate the no-surprises rule that makes executive communication credible."}]},{"key":"adoption/usage-analytics","sources":["Rodden, Hutchinson, Fu, 2010 — Measuring the User Experience on a Large Scale (HEART framework)","Kohavi, Tang, Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments"],"why":"After go-live the usual question is \"is anyone using it?\", and the usual answer is a login count or a\nrequest count on the API gateway. Both mislead. A request count goes up when one enthusiastic team uses\nthe tool heavily while forty others ignore it. It also goes up when users regenerate the same answer five\ntimes because the first four were wrong. Logins count people who opened the page, not people who got\nanything done. Neither has a denominator: 2,000 weekly users is a success among 2,500 eligible agents and\na failure among 40,000.\n\nAI features add a problem ordinary product analytics does not have. The output is probabilistic, so the\ninteresting behaviour comes *after* the response: did the user send the draft unchanged, rewrite half of\nit, copy one paragraph, or close the panel? Those downstream actions are the cheapest large-scale quality\nsignal you will ever get. Explicit thumbs-up/down buttons are clicked by a small, unrepresentative\nfraction of users, mostly when annoyed. The naive alternative is to read raw prompt logs or the tracing\nbackend. That fails for two reasons: traces are keyed by request, not by user journey, and copying raw\nprompts into an analytics warehouse spreads sensitive customer content to a system with broad access.","how":"### 1. Define the eligible population and the unit of value\nStart with who *could* use the feature (licensed seats, agents in the pilot queues) and what one unit of\nwork is (a ticket answered, a contract reviewed, a query resolved). Every rate needs these two: adoption\nis active users ÷ eligible users, not active users alone, and depth is units of work done with AI ÷ units\nof work done in total.\n\n### 2. Design an event schema around outcomes, not clicks\nA small, stable vocabulary beats hundreds of ad-hoc events. A typical AI-feature schema is:\n- **exposure**: the feature was shown or offered (needed for any funnel denominator);\n- **invocation**: the user deliberately asked for AI help;\n- **response**: the system returned output, carrying the `request_id` that joins to the trace;\n- **disposition**: accepted as-is, accepted after edit (with an edit-size measure), regenerated,\n  discarded, escalated to a human;\n- **explicit feedback**: thumbs, ratings, free-text reasons.\nEach event carries a pseudonymous user ID, account or tenant, feature name, prompt/model version and a\ntimestamp. The `request_id` is the design choice that matters most: it lets you go from \"acceptance\ndropped on Tuesday\" to the exact traces, prompt version and retrieved documents behind the dropped\nrequests, *without* storing content in the analytics store.\n\nEdit size deserves a precise definition, because \"edited\" alone lumps a fixed typo together with a full\nrewrite. Compute it in the client, where the draft and the sent text are both already present, for\nexample as `1 − similarity(draft, sent)` with a character- or token-level diff ratio, and emit only the\nnumber. Bucket it (0, under 10%, 10–50%, over 50%) so the dashboard shows the shape of the distribution;\nthe mean hides the difference between \"most drafts need a light touch\" and \"half are fine, half are\nrewritten from scratch\".\n\n### 3. Prefer implicit signals, calibrate them with explicit ones\nDisposition events are emitted for every response, so they avoid the self-selection bias of explicit\nratings. They are not bias-free: \"abandoned\" has to be inferred from a timeout or a closed panel, and\nusers copy text out in ways the UI may not see. They are also noisy: a user may accept a mediocre draft\nbecause fixing it is quicker than writing from scratch. Treat\n\"accepted unchanged\", \"edited by more than half\" and \"regenerated\" as proxies. Periodically check them\nagainst human-labelled samples, so you know what a 60% acceptance rate means for *this* workflow.\n\n### 4. Build the standard views\n- **Funnel**: eligible → exposed → tried once → used in 3+ distinct weeks. Where people drop out tells you\n  whether the problem is awareness, first experience or ongoing value.\n- **Cohort retention**: of users who first used it in week *w*, the share still using it in week *w+n*.\n  Retention that flattens above zero signals a habit; retention decaying toward zero is a novelty curve.\n- **Stickiness and depth**: weekly-active ÷ monthly-active, and AI-assisted share of all work items.\n- **Breadth**: active users by team, region or role, because adoption is usually lumpy.\n- **Quality proxies over time**: acceptance, edit size and regeneration rate, sliced by prompt version.\nFrameworks such as Google's HEART (Happiness, Engagement, Adoption, Retention, Task success) are a\nuseful checklist that the views above cover the whole journey.\n\n### 5. Check noise and mix before reading a change\nEvery rate on the dashboard is a proportion over a finite count, so it moves by chance. For a rate *p*\nover *n* events, the standard error is about `sqrt(p(1−p)/n)`, and for the difference between two\nperiods or two prompt versions it is about `sqrt(p₁(1−p₁)/n₁ + p₂(1−p₂)/n₂)`. Treat a move smaller than\nroughly two standard errors as unproven. Small pilots make this bite: at 50 drafts a week, acceptance\ncan swing ten points with nothing changed.\n\nThe second check is mix. An aggregate rate is a weighted average over segments, so it moves when the\nweights move even if no segment changes. If a new team with naturally harder tickets joins in the same\nweek a prompt ships, overall acceptance falls and the prompt gets blamed. Slice the change by team or\ncohort first: a real regression shows up *within* segments; a mix shift shows flat segments and a moving\ntotal.\n\n### 6. Keep it clean and lawful\nExclude internal, test and service accounts, or the vendor team's own testing inflates week one. Hash or\ntokenize user IDs. Keep prompt and response text in the access-controlled tracing store, not in analytics.\nAgree retention periods and works-council or employee-monitoring constraints with the customer *before*\ninstrumenting. In many organisations, per-employee productivity tracking is a legal and political issue,\nso aggregate to team level by default.","example":"Two Postgres queries over an `ai_events` table for a support-reply drafting assistant. The first computes\nweekly cohort retention on a deliberate action (`draft_requested`), excluding internal users; the second\ntracks what agents did with the drafts.\n\n```sql\nWITH active AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT e.user_id, date_trunc('week', e.ts)::date AS wk\n  FROM ai_events e JOIN users u ON u.user_id = e.user_id\n  WHERE e.feature = 'draft_reply'\n    AND e.event_type = 'draft_requested'      -- an intentional action, not an impression\n    AND NOT u.is_internal\n),\ncohort AS (SELECT user_id, min(wk) AS cohort_wk FROM active GROUP BY user_id),\nsize   AS (SELECT cohort_wk, count(*) AS n FROM cohort GROUP BY cohort_wk)\nSELECT c.cohort_wk,\n       (a.wk - c.cohort_wk) / 7                              AS weeks_since_first_use,\n       count(DISTINCT a.user_id)                             AS active_users,\n       round(count(DISTINCT a.user_id)::numeric / max(s.n), 3) AS retention\nFROM cohort c\nJOIN active a ON a.user_id = c.user_id\nJOIN size s   ON s.cohort_wk = c.cohort_wk\nGROUP BY c.cohort_wk, weeks_since_first_use\nORDER BY c.cohort_wk, weeks_since_first_use;\n\n-- Disposition of drafts per week, sliced by prompt version.\nSELECT date_trunc('week', ts)::date AS wk, prompt_version,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'draft_shown')          AS shown,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'draft_sent_unchanged') AS sent_as_is,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'draft_sent_edited')    AS sent_edited,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'draft_regenerated')    AS regenerated\nFROM ai_events\nWHERE feature = 'draft_reply'\nGROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY 1, 2;\n```\n\nReading them together is the point. Suppose (illustrative numbers) the week-4 retention of the first\ncohort is 0.35, and `sent_as_is / shown` fell from 0.42 to 0.30 the week prompt v7 shipped, over about\n500 drafts on each version. The standard error of the difference is about\n`sqrt(0.42·0.58/500 + 0.30·0.70/500) ≈ 0.03`, so a 12-point drop is roughly four standard errors: not\nnoise. With 50 drafts per version the same error would be about 0.095, and the drop would prove nothing.\nNext, slice by team: if every team's sent-as-is rate fell, it is the prompt; if only the newly onboarded\nbilling team is low and the others are flat, it is mix. Here every team fell, and regenerations rose,\nso you have a quality regression to chase in the traces by `request_id`, not an adoption problem.","tradeoffs":["Rich disposition events give unbiased coverage of every response, but they need front-end work in the customer's UI; when you cannot touch the UI, you fall back to sparse explicit feedback.","Per-user granularity enables cohort and funnel analysis, but it raises employee-monitoring concerns; where works councils or regulators object, team-level aggregates are the right default.","A small fixed event vocabulary keeps dashboards comparable across releases, at the cost of missing feature-specific nuance; add events only when a decision depends on them.","Storing content alongside events makes ad-hoc analysis easy but copies sensitive data widely; keep content in the tracing store and join by `request_id` unless the data is non-sensitive."],"pitfalls":["Counting requests instead of users and work items: the symptom is a rising usage chart while most eligible teams show zero activity in a breadth view.","Reporting adoption without a denominator: leadership celebrates \"1,200 users\" until someone asks how many were licensed and the answer is 9,000.","Leaving internal and test accounts in: week-one usage looks spectacular and then \"collapses\" when the vendor team stops testing.","Treating thumbs-down rate as quality: it moves with UI placement and annoyance, so it disagrees with acceptance and edit-size trends and with human review.","Blaming a prompt for a mix shift: overall acceptance drops in the week a new team onboards, yet every segment's rate is flat when sliced.","Turning acceptance rate into a target: drafts get shorter and blander, acceptance climbs, and downstream outcomes such as reopen rate or customer satisfaction slip.","Logging raw prompts into the product analytics tool: discovered at a security review, after customer data has spread to a system with broad access."],"when":"Instrument usage analytics before the pilot starts, not after someone asks for numbers. You cannot\nreconstruct disposition events afterwards, and the first weeks are when the funnel is most informative.\nUse it whenever you need to separate \"the system is wrong\" from \"people are not using it\" from \"people\nuse it but it doesn't help\". Those three diagnoses lead to engineering, training and scoping work\nrespectively. A minimal dashboard that covers those three diagnoses is: weekly active ÷ eligible,\nAI-assisted share of work items, week-4 cohort retention, and accepted-unchanged, edit-size and\nregeneration rates sliced by prompt version and team. Do not use it as a substitute for outcome measurement: high usage proves engagement, not value\n(see adoption and value-realization measurement). In interviews, expect to design the event schema for\nan AI feature and say which implicit signals you would trust and why.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Emits disposition events with a `request_id` that joins to traces, versions every prompt in the event payload, and writes the cohort and funnel queries.","architect":"Decides where content lives versus where behavioural events live, sets the pseudonymisation and retention policy, and standardises the event vocabulary across features.","fde":"Negotiates what may be tracked inside the customer's environment, gets UI hooks into their tools, and reads the breadth view to find the teams that need help."},"check":{"q":"A customer's AI drafting tool shows weekly requests up 40% month over month, and the sponsor wants\nto declare success. What would you check before agreeing, and why?","a":"I would check whether the growth is broad or concentrated, and whether it reflects value or\nretries. First, normalise by eligible users and look at breadth: are more teams active, or is one team\nusing it harder? Second, check requests per completed work item and the regeneration rate. A jump in\nretries after a prompt or model change inflates requests while quality falls. Third, check cohort\nretention, to see whether new users stick or decay after a novelty week. Fourth, check disposition:\nsent-as-is and edit-size trends. Only broad, retained usage with stable or improving acceptance supports\nthe claim. Even then it shows engagement, and value still needs an outcome comparison."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are disposition events (accepted, edited, regenerated, discarded) usually preferred over thumbs ratings as the main quality signal?","options":["They are more accurate than human judgement of output quality","They are recorded for every response, so they avoid the self-selection bias of the few users who click ratings","They do not require any changes to the user interface","They can be computed from the model's log-probabilities alone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Implicit dispositions cover every response. Ratings come from a small, skewed subset, often annoyed users. Dispositions are still noisy proxies and need calibrating against labelled samples, and they do require UI instrumentation."},{"q":"What is the main reason to carry a `request_id` on analytics events instead of copying prompt and response text into the analytics store?","options":["Analytics tools cannot store long text fields","It makes the cohort retention query run faster","It lets you drill from a behavioural anomaly to the exact trace while keeping sensitive content in the access-controlled tracing store","It is required by the OpenTelemetry specification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The join key gives you both views without duplicating customer content into a widely accessible system. Speed and storage limits are not the reason, and no telemetry standard mandates this specific design."},{"q":"A cohort chart shows each weekly cohort's usage falling steadily toward zero by week six. What does this most likely indicate?","options":["A logging bug that drops events from older users","A novelty effect: people try the feature but do not find enough ongoing value to form a habit","Healthy adoption, because new cohorts keep arriving each week","That the eligible population was defined too broadly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Retention that decays toward zero instead of flattening is the classic novelty curve. New cohorts arriving can hide it in a total-users chart, which is exactly why cohort views exist."}]},{"key":"adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","sources":["Kohavi, Tang, Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments","Angrist, Pischke, 2009 — Mostly Harmless Econometrics"],"why":"Every AI deployment is sold on a projected value: an ROI model that says something like \"25% faster\nclaims handling across 200 adjusters\". Ninety days later someone has to say how much of that showed up.\nWithout a deliberate measurement design, three things go wrong.\n\nFirst, adoption gets reported as value. \"80% of adjusters used it this month\" says nothing about whether\nclaims got faster. The tool could be used and ignored, or used and slower. Second, before/after\ncomparisons claim credit for everything that changed at the same time. A new intake form, a seasonal dip\nin claim complexity, or a staffing change all move the number. Users who adopt early also tend to be the\nfast ones, so comparing adopters with non-adopters measures who chose the tool, not what the tool did.\nThird, self-reported time savings are routinely optimistic. Hours \"saved\" that are not redeployed to\nbacklog, volume or reduced overtime never reach a P&L.\n\nThe forcing problem is attribution under business conditions. You rarely get a clean lab experiment.\nYou still owe the sponsor a defensible number, a range around it, and an explanation of the gap between\nprojected and realized value. The explanation decides the next move: more training, product fixes, or\nre-scoping.","how":"### 1. Write the value equation before go-live\nMake the ROI model's chain explicit so each link can be measured separately:\n\n```\nrealized value ≈ eligible volume × adoption share × effect per assisted unit × conversion − run cost\n```\n\n*Adoption share* is the fraction of eligible work actually done with AI assistance, not the fraction of\nusers who logged in. *Effect per assisted unit* is the change in the outcome metric (minutes, error rate,\nrevenue) on that work. *Conversion* is whether the gain becomes money or capacity: freed hours absorbed\ninto backlog, avoided hires, reduced overtime. Decomposing it this way matters *because* a shortfall in\nthe total is useless on its own. You need to know which factor fell short to know what to fix.\n\n### 2. Define adoption as a ladder, not a number\nMeasure breadth (share of eligible users active weekly), depth (share of their eligible work items\nAI-assisted) and durability (cohort retention past the novelty period). Set targets for each by date,\nfor example 30/60/90 days. Adoption is the lever the delivery team controls most directly: through\ntraining, workflow integration and change management.\n\n### 3. Choose the strongest attribution design the situation allows\nFrom strongest to weakest:\n- **Randomised holdout**: a random subset of users or cases keeps the old process. This is clean but\n  politically hard when the tool is popular.\n- **Staggered rollout with difference-in-differences**: groups go live at different dates, and you compare\n  each group's change with the change in groups not yet live. This removes shared trends such as\n  seasonality or a process change, provided the groups would have moved in parallel without the tool.\n  Check that the pre-period trends really were parallel. With many groups going live at different dates,\n  compare each group only against groups not yet live; a single pooled regression that also uses\n  already-live groups as controls can be biased when the effect grows over time.\n- **Matched comparison**: compare against similar teams that did not get the tool. Selection bias remains.\n- **Before/after against a baseline**: valid only if nothing else changed. Always report it as the weakest\n  evidence.\n- **Self-report surveys**: useful for satisfaction and for *why*, but not for the size of the effect.\nUse an intention-to-treat comparison (all work in the live group, used or not) when you want the\ndeployment's real effect. Dividing it by the adoption share gives a rough per-assisted-unit effect,\nunder the assumption that non-assisted work did not change.\n\n### 4. Put a range on the estimate honestly\nThe tempting error bar is the one computed from thousands of claims, which comes out tiny. It is wrong,\nbecause the treatment was assigned to *groups* (regions, teams), not to individual claims, and groups\ndrift relative to each other for reasons that have nothing to do with the tool. With two regions you\nhave, in effect, one comparison. Two practical ways to get a realistic range:\n- **Placebo tests:** run the same DiD calculation on pre-launch weeks, pretending a go-live happened when\n  it did not. The spread of those fake \"effects\" shows how much the groups wander apart on their own; a\n  real effect has to stand clearly outside it.\n- **Unit-level variation:** when the rollout is staggered over many teams, compute the estimate per team\n  (or bootstrap by resampling teams, not claims) and report the spread across teams.\nAlso watch the effect over time. Week-6 numbers can include novelty and extra attention from the\nlaunch team, so re-check the per-assisted-unit effect at the 90-day point before it goes into a renewal.\n\n### 5. Pair every value metric with guardrails\nSpeed gains that raise error, rework, complaint or compliance-exception rates are not value. Agree the\nguardrail thresholds with the customer in advance, so \"faster but worse\" is caught by the definition and\nnot argued about afterwards.\n\n### 6. Run a value-realization cadence\nAt each checkpoint, report projected versus realized value by factor. Give ranges, not point estimates,\nand state the attribution method, the assumptions and the guardrail status. Assign each shortfall to an\nowner: adoption gaps to enablement, effect gaps to engineering, conversion gaps to the customer's\noperations leadership.","example":"A claims-intake assistant for 200 eligible adjusters, each handling about 45 claims a week (9,000 claims a\nweek in total). The baseline measured over four weeks before launch was 40 minutes of handling time per\nclaim. The ROI model projected a 25% cut (10 minutes) on assisted claims at 90% adoption:\n10 × 0.9 × 9,000 = 81,000 minutes ≈ **1,350 hours a week**. All numbers are illustrative.\n\nRegion A goes live in week 0 and region B in week 6, which gives a natural comparison. Over weeks 0–6:\n- Region A, all claims: 40 → 32 minutes (−8).\n- Region B, not yet live: 41 → 38 minutes (−3). A simplified intake form shipped company-wide in week 2.\n- Difference-in-differences: −8 − (−3) = **−5 minutes per claim** attributable to the assistant (the\n  pre-launch trends of the two regions were flat and parallel).\n- Placebo check: the same calculation over pre-launch weeks, with a pretend go-live, gave \"effects\"\n  between −1 and +1 minute. So the honest report is about −4 to −6 minutes, not −5.00 ± 0.1 from the\n  claim-level standard error.\n\nThe naive before/after for adopters alone said −9 minutes. It included the form change and the fact that\nfast adjusters adopted first.\n\nAdoption in region A at week 6: 65% of adjusters weekly active, using it on 70% of their claims, so the\nadoption share is about 0.65 × 0.70 ≈ 0.46 of claims (assuming similar volumes per adjuster). The implied\neffect per assisted claim is 5 ÷ 0.46 ≈ **11 minutes** (about 9–13 across the range), in line with the\nprojected 10.\n\nRealized value at full rollout, if region B reaches the same adoption as region A: 5 × 9,000 = 45,000\nminutes ≈ **750 hours a week** (600–900 across the placebo range), about 56% of projection (roughly\n45–65%). The decomposition shows the gap is adoption (0.46 against a planned 0.9), not model quality, so the next investment is enablement: the 35%\nof adjusters who do not use it, and the 30% of claims that active users still handle unassisted. Guardrail: claim reopen rate moved from 4.1% to 4.3%,\ninside the agreed ±0.5-point band. Conversion is checked separately: the customer's backlog fell, so the\nfreed hours were absorbed rather than lost to slack.","tradeoffs":["Randomised holdouts give the most credible effect estimate, but they withhold a useful tool from some users; when politics forbid it, a staggered rollout is the usual compromise.","Intention-to-treat estimates are honest about real deployment impact but understate per-use effect; divide by adoption share only when non-assisted work plausibly did not change.","Reporting ranges and assumptions protects credibility with finance, but executives may prefer one number; give the conservative end as the headline when the decision is a renewal.","Measuring business outcomes takes weeks to accumulate signal, so you also track leading indicators (adoption, acceptance) that move faster but prove nothing about value on their own."],"pitfalls":["Reporting usage as value: the dashboard shows high weekly actives, but the finance team finds no change in cost per case and stops trusting the AI team's numbers.","Before/after without a comparison group: a large effect appears that coincides with a seasonal dip or a process change, and it shrinks or reverses the following quarter.","Comparing adopters with non-adopters: the effect looks huge because self-selected adopters were already the strongest performers, which shows up as a gap that predates go-live.","Counting hours saved that are never redeployed: projected savings appear in slides, but headcount, overtime and backlog are unchanged, so the budget owner sees nothing.","Error bars computed from individual claims when the rollout was assigned by region: the reported range is implausibly tight, and the next region's result lands far outside it.","Skipping guardrails: handling time improves while rework or complaints rise, discovered later through escalations instead of the measurement plan."],"when":"Set up value-realization measurement during scoping, when you agree the baseline and the ROI model, and\nstart reporting at the first 30-day checkpoint. It is mandatory for anything sold on a business case,\nfor renewals and expansions, and for internal platform teams defending budget. For small internal\nproductivity tools, a lighter version (adoption ladder plus a baseline comparison) may be proportionate.\nMeasure adoption weekly. Measure value monthly or per checkpoint, once enough volume has accumulated.\nIn interviews, expect a \"how would you prove this worked?\" question. The strong answer names the\ncomparison group, decomposes the gap, and names the guardrails.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the pipelines that join usage events to the customer's outcome data, and computes intention-to-treat and per-assisted-unit effects reproducibly.","architect":"Designs the rollout so attribution is possible (staggered regions, holdout cohorts) and owns the value equation that links system metrics to the business case.","fde":"Runs the 30/60/90 value reviews with the sponsor, explains shortfalls by factor, and turns the biggest gap into an action plan with named owners."},"check":{"q":"Ninety days in, realized value is 40% of the ROI projection. The sponsor asks whether the model is\nunderperforming. How do you answer?","a":"I decompose the shortfall instead of guessing. Using the value equation, I check adoption share\n(breadth × depth) against the plan, the effect per assisted unit from the comparison design, and whether\nfreed capacity was converted. If adoption is at 45% against a 90% plan while the per-assisted-unit effect\nmatches projection, the model is fine and the gap is enablement or workflow fit. If adoption is on plan\nbut the effect is small, it is a quality or task-fit problem for engineering. I state the attribution\nmethod, give a range and confirm the guardrails held. Then I propose the specific next lever with an owner and date."},"quiz":[{"q":"A staggered rollout shows the live region's handling time fell 8 minutes and the not-yet-live region's fell 3 minutes over the same weeks. What is the difference-in-differences estimate, and what does it assume?","options":["8 minutes, assuming the not-yet-live region is irrelevant","5 minutes, assuming both regions would have followed parallel trends without the tool","11 minutes, assuming the two changes add up","5 minutes, assuming adoption in the live region was 100%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"DiD subtracts the shared trend (−3) from the treated change (−8), giving −5. Its key assumption is parallel trends. Adoption below 100% does not invalidate it; it makes the result an intention-to-treat estimate."},{"q":"Why is comparing outcomes of adopters versus non-adopters within the same team a weak attribution design?","options":["Because the two groups use different model versions","Because adopters generate more analytics events","Because users choose to adopt, so differences may reflect who adopted rather than what the tool did","Because non-adopters cannot have baseline measurements"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Self-selection is the problem. Early adopters are often already faster or more engaged, so their advantage predates the tool. Randomisation or staggered rollout breaks that link."},{"q":"Realized value is half the projection, adoption share is half the planned level, and the per-assisted-unit effect matches the projection. What should the next investment target?","options":["Fine-tuning the model to improve output quality","Enablement and workflow integration to raise adoption among non-users","Revising the baseline measurement upward","Adding guardrail metrics to the dashboard"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The decomposition puts the entire gap in adoption, because each assisted unit delivers as projected. Improving the model would raise an effect that is already on target and leave the real shortfall untouched."}]},{"key":"adoption/continuous-improvement","sources":[],"why":"A system that passed acceptance on launch day keeps meeting inputs nobody tested. New users phrase things\ndifferently, the knowledge base changes, the vendor updates the model behind an alias, and the business\nadds products the prompts never mention. Quality does not stay where acceptance left it. It drifts, and\nusers quietly route around a tool they stop trusting.\n\nThe naive response is reactive patching. A complaint arrives, someone edits the prompt until that example\nworks, and the change ships. That fails in three ways. First, LLM behaviour is coupled: an instruction\nadded to fix French users' replies can change tone or format for everyone else, and without a regression\nsuite nobody notices until the next complaint. Second, the loudest stakeholder's anecdote gets fixed\nbefore the failure that hits two hundred users a week, because nothing measures frequency. Third, nobody\ncan say whether the system is getting better overall. The team is busy but the acceptance rate is flat.\n\nContinuous improvement replaces that with a loop that has a measurable input (classified failures), a\nmeasurable gate (no regression on a frozen suite) and a measurable output (the targeted bucket shrinks\nin production). It is the plan-do-check-act cycle from quality management, with test-driven bug fixing\nas the \"check\" step, adapted to systems whose outputs are probabilistic and whose dependencies change\nunderneath them.","how":"### 1. Observe\nPull signals from several sources, because each sees different failures: disposition events from usage\nanalytics (regenerations, heavy edits, abandons), explicit feedback, escalations to humans, trace-level\nerrors (tool failures, timeouts, empty retrievals), incidents, and periodic human review of a random\nsample. The random sample matters *because* every other source is biased toward failures that users\nnotice and bother to report.\n\n### 2. Classify\nGroup failures into a stable taxonomy through error analysis: read real traces and name the failure\nmodes (\"cited superseded policy\", \"wrong language\", \"tool called with wrong account ID\"). Use an LLM\nclassifier to scale the labelling once the categories are stable, and spot-check it. Counts per bucket\nper week are the input to everything that follows.\n\n### 3. Prioritise\nA simple score is weekly count × severity ÷ effort, with a hard override: anything that is harmful,\nnon-compliant or loses money gets fixed first regardless of its score. Use the override *because* a\npure frequency score will always rank a frequent cosmetic issue above a rare one that ends the contract.\n\n### 4. Fix at the cheapest layer that works\nDiagnose which layer owns the failure before touching anything. In rough order of cost: UX or training\n(the user asked for something out of scope), prompt or instructions, retrieval and data (wrong, stale or\nmissing documents), tools and integrations, guardrails, model choice, and fine-tuning. Many \"model\nfailures\" turn out to be retrieval or data failures, and a prompt cannot fix a document that is not in\nthe index.\n\n### 5. Encode before you fix\nAdd the failing production cases, de-identified as needed, to the regression suite *before* changing the\nsystem. This proves the suite catches the failure, and keeps it caught after later changes.\n\n### 6. Gate, release, verify\nRun the full frozen suite on the candidate. Require improvement on the targeted slice and no regression\nbeyond noise on every other slice. \"Noise\" needs a number, for two reasons. Slices are small, so a pass\nrate over *n* cases has a standard error of about `sqrt(p(1−p)/n)`; and LLM outputs and LLM judges vary\nbetween runs even on identical inputs. Because baseline and candidate run on the *same* cases, compare\nthem paired: count cases that flipped pass→fail (*b*) and fail→pass (*c*). McNemar's test treats\n`(b − c)² / (b + c)` above about 3.84 as a change unlikely to be chance at the 5% level (with only a\nhandful of flips, use the exact binomial version instead). For flaky\ncases, run each several times and score the majority, or the gate will block and pass at random.\n\nShip behind a canary or shadow deployment, then check online that the bucket shrank. Use a *rate* (failures\nper 1,000 conversations) compared with the bucket's own pre-release weeks, not a raw count, because\ntraffic growth makes a fixed bucket look bigger. Version every prompt, retrieval config and model, so a\nchange in production metrics can be attributed to a specific release.\n\n### 7. Run it on a cadence\nA weekly triage and a fixed release train keep the loop moving without turning every complaint into an\nemergency. External changes go through the same gate: a vendor model update, a re-indexed knowledge base,\nor a new user group. Close the loop with reporters (\"you flagged X, fixed in release 14\"), because\nvisible responses keep feedback rates up.","example":"A weekly triage and release gate for a support assistant. Bucket counts come from classified production\nfeedback. The ranking applies the severity override, and the gate compares per-slice pass rates on the\nsame frozen regression suite.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Bucket:\n    name: str\n    weekly_count: int        # classified production failures this week\n    severity: int            # 1 cosmetic, 3 wrong answer, 5 harmful / non-compliant\n    effort_days: float\n    layer: str               # prompt | retrieval | tool | guardrail | model\n\ndef rank_key(b: Bucket):\n    # Severity-5 items jump the queue; the rest are ranked by count x severity / effort.\n    return (b.severity >= 5, b.weekly_count * b.severity / b.effort_days)\n\nbuckets = [\n    Bucket(\"wrong date format\", 300, 1, 0.5, \"prompt\"),           # score 600\n    Bucket(\"cites superseded policy\", 140, 3, 3.0, \"retrieval\"),  # score 140\n    Bucket(\"replies in English to French users\", 60, 2, 1.0, \"prompt\"),  # 120\n    Bucket(\"promises refund outside policy\", 12, 5, 2.0, \"guardrail\"),   # 30, but severity 5\n]\nfor b in sorted(buckets, key=rank_key, reverse=True):\n    print(f\"{b.name:38} layer={b.layer}\")\n\ndef release_ok(base: dict, cand: dict, target: str, tol: float = 0.02) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Pass rates per slice on the same frozen suite; tol should reflect slice sample size.\"\"\"\n    improved = cand[target] > base[target]\n    no_regression = all(cand[s] >= base[s] - tol for s in base)\n    return improved and no_regression\n\nbase = {\"refunds\": 0.81, \"billing\": 0.93, \"french\": 0.88, \"shipping\": 0.95}\ncand = {\"refunds\": 0.97, \"billing\": 0.94, \"french\": 0.84, \"shipping\": 0.95}\nprint(release_ok(base, cand, \"refunds\"))   # False: french fell 4 points, beyond tolerance\n```\n\nThe refund guardrail goes first despite the lowest score. The candidate fixes refunds but is blocked,\nbecause the fix cost the French slice four points. That is exactly the regression that\nreactive patching would have shipped.\n\nIs four points real or noise? Suppose (illustratively) the French slice has 250 cases: 0.88 → 0.84 is\nten net cases. Paired, say 13 flipped pass→fail and 3 fail→pass: `(13 − 3)² / 16 ≈ 6.3`, above 3.84, so\nblock. Had the slice held 25 cases, four points would be one case, and the right response is to rerun or\ngrow the slice, not to trust a fixed 2-point tolerance. In this scenario the cause was that the new\nrefund instruction (\"always state the policy in English legal terms\") overrode the language rule, which\nthe trace of a flipped case shows directly.","tradeoffs":["A fast release train shortens time-to-fix but makes attribution harder; batch fewer changes per release when you need to know which change moved the metric.","Fixing at the prompt layer is quick and cheap, but it accumulates brittle instructions; once the same bucket returns after several prompt fixes, move the fix to retrieval, tools or fine-tuning.","A growing regression suite catches more, but it gets slower and costlier to run with an LLM judge; prune duplicate and obsolete cases, and tier the suite into fast and full runs.","Fixing one customer's failure in shared prompts helps everyone but risks others' behaviour; when the failure is truly customer-specific, put the fix in that customer's configuration and suite."],"pitfalls":["Whack-a-mole prompt edits without a suite: the symptom is complaints you already fixed reappearing a few releases later.","Prioritising by who complained loudest: the dashboard shows a large, steady bucket that has gone untouched for weeks while small anecdotes get fixed.","Overfitting to the regression suite: offline pass rates climb every release while production acceptance and edit rates stay flat.","Tracking bucket counts instead of rates: a fixed failure appears to \"come back\" in the month traffic doubles, while its rate per 1,000 conversations is still down.","A gate that ignores run-to-run variance: the same candidate passes on Monday and fails on Tuesday, and the team learns to rerun until green.","Unversioned changes: production quality shifts and nobody can tell whether the prompt edit, the re-index or the vendor's model update caused it.","Feedback that disappears into a backlog: feedback volume drops over time because users learn that reporting changes nothing."],"when":"Run this loop for any AI system in production with real users, starting in hypercare and continuing for\nthe system's life. The cadence can slow as the failure buckets shrink, but it should not stop, because\ninputs and dependencies keep changing. It is less useful during early prototyping, when the design itself\nis still moving and a frozen suite would freeze the wrong behaviour. Measure the loop itself: weekly\nfailure rate by bucket, time from report to fix, regression escapes per release, and production\nacceptance trend. In interviews, describe the loop end to end and stress the gate and the online check.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the classifier and the gate, adds failing cases to the suite before each fix, and versions prompts and configs so production changes are attributable.","architect":"Owns the release process: eval gates, canary or shadow stages, which changes need full suites, and how customer-specific fixes are isolated from shared behaviour.","fde":"Runs the weekly triage with the customer, translates their complaints into failure buckets, and reports back which fixes shipped and what moved."},"check":{"q":"After six weeks of prompt fixes driven by user complaints, the offline suite pass rate is up from\n82% to 94%, but production acceptance is flat. What is likely happening and what do you change?","a":"The suite and production have diverged. The suite probably holds only the complaint cases, so the\nfixes overfit to them, while the bulk of production failures were never classified. Unreported failures\ncould also be growing, from drift or new users. I would review a random production sample to rebuild the\nfailure taxonomy, add representative cases rather than only complained-about ones, and check whether\nprompt patches have accumulated into conflicting instructions. Then I would prioritise by measured bucket\nfrequency and severity. Each release would be validated by an online check that the targeted bucket\nshrank, not only by offline pass rate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why add the failing production cases to the regression suite before implementing the fix?","options":["It makes the release pipeline run faster","It proves the suite detects the failure, and it keeps the failure detected after later changes","It is needed so the LLM judge can learn from the examples","It prevents the vendor from changing the model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encoding first is the test-driven pattern: a test that fails before the fix and passes after shows the suite actually covers the failure. That protects against regressions from future changes."},{"q":"A bucket of \"promises refunds outside policy\" has 12 weekly cases, while \"wrong date format\" has 300. Which prioritisation is sound?","options":["Date format first, because frequency × severity ÷ effort gives it the higher score","Refund promises first, because harmful or non-compliant failures override the frequency score","Whichever is cheaper to fix, because effort dominates","Neither, until the counts are statistically significant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pure scores rank frequent cosmetic issues above rare costly ones. A severity override puts harmful, non-compliant or money-losing failures first, then the score orders the rest."},{"q":"A candidate release raises the targeted slice from 81% to 97% but drops another slice by 4 points, beyond the agreed tolerance. What should happen?","options":["Ship it, because the average pass rate across slices went up","Ship it and add the dropped slice to next week's triage","Block it and rework the fix, because the gate requires no regression beyond noise on any slice","Widen the tolerance so the release passes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Averages hide regressions for specific user groups. The per-slice no-regression rule exists to catch exactly this kind of coupled side effect before it reaches production."}]},{"key":"adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","sources":[],"why":"Forward-deployed work ends. The FDE moves to the next account, and the system has to keep working\nwithout them. The common failure is not a dramatic outage on the day they leave. It is slow decay over\nthe following months. Nobody on the customer side knows why retrieval filters by region, so someone\n\"simplifies\" it. Nobody can run the eval suite, so a vendor model update ships unchecked. An alert fires\ninto a channel nobody watches. A credential expires that was issued to the FDE's personal account. Six\nmonths later the customer concludes \"the AI didn't work\", and the renewal goes with it.\n\nThe naive handoff is a document dump: a wiki page, an architecture diagram and a recorded walkthrough in\nthe final week. It fails because operating an AI system is a skill, not a body of facts. Reading about\ntriaging a quality regression does not prepare someone to do it at 2 a.m. AI systems also carry more tacit\nknowledge than ordinary services. Why a prompt is phrased the way it is, which eval slices are noisy,\nwhich failures are known and acceptable: much of that lives only in the builder's head. The forcing\nproblem is moving that knowledge and the matching responsibility to people who will be accountable for\nthe system after the builder is gone.","how":"### 1. Define what is being handed off\nSplit ownership into explicit areas, because each needs different skills and often different people:\n- **Run**: on-call, alerts, incident response, capacity and cost budgets.\n- **Change**: prompt and config edits, re-indexing, dependency and model upgrades, all through the\n  release gate.\n- **Quality**: the regression suite, failure triage, and the continuous-improvement cadence.\n- **Access and accounts**: service identities, secrets, data permissions, audit, and who owns and pays\n  for the model-provider and cloud accounts (a vendor-billed API account is a dependency that outlives\n  the engagement unless it is moved).\n- **Vendor interface**: what the vendor still supports after handoff, how to escalate, and with what SLAs.\nWrite this as a responsibility matrix (RACI) that both sides sign. An unclear boundary turns into\nsupport tickets the vendor bounces back and forth with the customer.\n\n### 2. Check the receiving team is real\nConfirm named people, allocated time and the needed skills before planning the transfer. A handoff to a\nteam with no capacity is a handoff to nobody. If the skills are missing, the honest options are training\nplus a longer transition, or a managed-service arrangement.\n\nPlan for at least two people per ownership area. If every exit task is done by the one engineer who\npaired with the FDE, the single point of failure has only moved from the vendor to the customer, and it\nreappears the first time that person is on leave or changes job. A simple skills matrix (areas down the\nside, named people across, a mark when someone has *done* the task) makes the gap visible early.\n\n### 3. Make the artifacts operable, not just readable\nMove infrastructure-as-code, prompts and configs into the customer's repositories and CI. Route dashboards\nand alerts to the customer's on-call tooling. Write runbooks keyed to specific alerts. Make the eval suite\nrunnable in the customer's pipeline. Re-issue every credential to customer-owned service identities.\nWrite decision records for the non-obvious choices, because the \"why\" is what stops a well-meaning\nsimplification from undoing a fix. Hand over a known-issues list and the open backlog.\n\n### 4. Transfer responsibility in stages\nUse a graduated sequence: the FDE does and the customer watches; they work as a pair; the customer does\nand the FDE watches (reverse shadowing); the customer works alone with the FDE as backup; exit. Each stage\nmoves the operator role one step, *because* people build operational skill by doing the task with a safety\nnet, not by reading about it.\n\n### 5. Exit on demonstrated capability\nExit criteria are tasks with evidence. For example, the customer team shipped a prompt change through the\ngate, ran the suite and correctly interpreted a regression, resolved a game-day incident using the\nrunbook, rotated a secret, and ran a weekly triage without help. A date can be a target, but the criteria\ndecide. Each criterion should be checkable by someone other than the FDE (a merged pull request, a gate\nreport, an incident ticket), because the FDE is the person with the strongest incentive to declare\nvictory on schedule.\n\n### 6. Taper, then check in\nKeep a defined hypercare window with reducing FDE involvement. Then schedule 30- and 90-day check-ins on\nthe same adoption and quality metrics, so post-handoff decay shows up as a trend and not as a churn surprise.\nTrack escalations back to the vendor per month as well: a falling count means ownership has moved; a\nflat or rising one means the customer is still operating the system through the vendor's people.","example":"A contract-review assistant built by a two-person FDE team is handed to the customer's platform team\n(three engineers and one operations analyst) over six weeks. The timeline is illustrative.\n\n- **Weeks 1–2 (FDE does, customer watches):** The RACI is agreed. Repos, Terraform and the prompt registry\n  move into the customer's GitHub organisation. The FDE re-issues the model-API key and the document-store\n  credentials to customer service accounts, and the personal keys are revoked. Alerts route to the\n  customer's PagerDuty rota with the FDE as secondary.\n- **Weeks 3–4 (pair, then reverse shadow):** A customer engineer makes a real change: adding the new\n  indemnity clause type to the extraction prompt. They add twelve labelled contracts to the suite first,\n  run the gate, see the \"governing law\" slice drop three points, fix the instruction conflict, and ship\n  behind the canary. The analyst runs the weekly failure triage while the FDE observes.\n- **Week 5 (customer alone, FDE backup):** Game day. The FDE injects a failure that empties retrieval\n  results for one business unit. The on-call engineer finds it from the alert and the runbook in\n  22 minutes, traces it to a revoked index permission, and restores it. The runbook gets two corrections.\n- **Week 6 (exit review):** Each criterion is checked against evidence: change shipped through the gate,\n  incident resolved, secret rotated, triage run unaided, decision records reviewed. The skills matrix shows\n  each task was done by only one engineer, and one item fails outright: nobody has done a vendor\n  model-version upgrade. Exit is moved a week. In that week the customer runs the upgrade with the suite,\n  and a second engineer repeats the gated change and the secret rotation, before sign-off. Check-ins are booked for day 30 and day 90.","tradeoffs":["A staged, criteria-based transfer costs weeks of FDE time, but it avoids decay after departure; a shorter handoff is reasonable only for simple, stable systems with experienced receiving teams.","Handing off full ownership frees the vendor, but it suits customers with real platform capability; when the customer lacks AI skills or the system still changes weekly, a managed-service arrangement is safer.","Adopting the customer's tooling (their CI, monitoring and ticketing) makes the system theirs, but it costs migration effort and loses vendor-standard dashboards; the trade flips toward vendor tooling when the vendor will keep operating the system.","Exhaustive documentation feels safe but goes stale quickly; short runbooks keyed to alerts plus decision records for non-obvious choices are maintained more often."],"pitfalls":["Credentials tied to the FDE's personal accounts: the symptom is an outage weeks after departure, when the account is deprovisioned or a personal API key expires.","A last-week document dump: the receiving team cannot answer basic \"what do I do when X alerts\" questions at the exit review, or nobody holds one.","Handing off to a team with no allocated capacity: alerts go unacknowledged and the triage cadence quietly stops within a month.","Undocumented design rationale: the customer \"simplifies\" a deliberate choice, such as a retrieval filter or prompt constraint, and an old failure returns.","An eval suite that only runs on the FDE's laptop: the first vendor model upgrade after handoff ships untested and quality shifts.","Every exit task done by one customer engineer: the system runs fine until that person leaves or is on holiday, then the next incident takes days.","A vague support boundary: tickets bounce between vendor support and the customer team, and resolution times climb."],"when":"Plan the handoff at the start of the engagement, not at the end. Who will own the system should shape\nearly choices: which repos, which CI, which monitoring stack, and whose identities. Use the full staged\nprocess for any system with real users, consequential outputs, or ongoing change. For a throwaway POC,\na short readme is enough. Measure success after handoff, not at the exit meeting: incident time-to-resolve\nby the customer team, changes shipped through the gate, triage continuity, and quality trends at 30 and\n90 days. In interviews, the signal is treating handoff as transfer of responsibility with exit criteria.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Moves code, prompts and infrastructure into customer-owned repos and identities, and makes the eval suite and gates runnable in the customer's CI without vendor-only tooling.","architect":"Draws the ownership boundary and support model, decides between full handoff and managed service, and keeps the design rationale in decision records.","fde":"Runs the graduated transfer, designs the game day, judges readiness against evidence, and negotiates a later exit when a criterion is not met."},"check":{"q":"The engagement contract ends in two weeks, but the customer team has not yet shipped a change\nthrough the release gate on its own. What do you do?","a":"I would not treat the contract date as proof of readiness. First, I make the gap visible to both\nsponsors: which exit criteria have evidence and which do not, and what risk each open item carries. In\nthe two weeks, I prioritise the highest-risk missing capability, usually a gated change and an incident\ndrill, by having the customer do them with me as backup. If they still are not ready, I propose\noptions: a short paid extension, a defined post-handoff support window with named escalation, or a\nmanaged-service arrangement for the parts they cannot yet own. Then the sponsor chooses with the risk\nwritten down."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are handoff exit criteria best expressed as tasks the customer team has performed?","options":["Because contracts legally require task-based acceptance","Because operating an AI system is a skill, and only doing tasks shows the team can handle them unaided","Because tasks are easier to schedule than reviews","Because documentation cannot describe AI systems accurately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reading runbooks does not prove someone can triage a regression or resolve an incident. Demonstrated tasks with evidence do. Dates and documents are inputs to readiness, not proof of it."},{"q":"Weeks after an FDE leaves, the assistant stops working with authentication errors to the model API. What handoff step most likely was missed?","options":["Moving prompts into the customer's prompt registry","Running a game-day incident drill","Re-issuing credentials to customer-owned service identities and revoking personal ones","Writing decision records for the retrieval design"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Credentials issued to an individual's account break when that account is deprovisioned or the key expires. Re-issuing them to customer service identities is a core handoff step."},{"q":"When is a managed-service arrangement a better choice than a full handoff?","options":["When the customer lacks the skills or capacity to operate the system and it still changes frequently","When the customer has a mature platform team with on-call rotations","Whenever the system uses a third-party model API","When the documentation is complete"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Full ownership needs a capable, staffed receiving team. Without one, or while the system is still changing quickly, the vendor operating it avoids handing responsibility to nobody."}]},{"key":"adoption/feature-request-triage","sources":[],"why":"A deployed AI system attracts asks constantly: in workshops, in Slack, from the sponsor, and from end\nusers during training sessions. An FDE hears dozens a week. Two naive responses both fail. Saying yes in\nthe field (\"we can add that\") wins goodwill this week. It also produces promises product never made,\ncustom branches that make every upgrade painful, and a roadmap set by one account. Forwarding everything\nto the product team unprocessed buries real signal in noise: twenty differently worded requests for the\nsame capability look like twenty small asks instead of one large one.\n\nAI products add a twist. A large share of asks are not features at all. \"Make it more accurate on\nforeign-exchange questions\" is a quality failure that belongs in the continuous-improvement loop with a\nregression case, not in a backlog for next quarter. \"Let it handle bulk uploads\" may already exist, and\nthe real need is training. \"Guarantee it never makes a mistake\" is a model capability limit that needs an\nexpectation reset and a human-review design, not engineering work. Misrouting these is costly: users keep\nsuffering a quality bug while it sits in a product queue, or product time goes to building what already\nexists.","how":"### 1. Capture the problem, not the solution\nRecord each request in a standard form: who asked (role and account), the job they are trying to get done,\nthe current workaround, what happens if nothing changes (blocks a deal, risks renewal, costs time), how\noften it occurs, and a concrete example. Keep asking \"what would that let you do?\" to get from \"add an\nExcel export button\" to \"finance reconciles AI answers against the ledger monthly\". You need the problem\n*because* clustering and solution design both depend on it; the customer's proposed solution is one\noption among several.\n\n### 2. Classify what the ask really is\n- **Quality failure:** the system already tries to do this and gets it wrong. Route it to failure triage,\n  add a regression case, and fix it through the release gate.\n- **Usage or knowledge gap:** the capability exists. Route it to enablement, and fix the docs or UI if\n  many people miss it.\n- **Configuration:** achievable with existing prompts, knowledge sources, settings or permissions. The field\n  team does it.\n- **Customer-specific extension:** a real need unique to this customer, such as an integration with their\n  ERP. Build it on a supported extension point, never as a fork.\n- **Product gap:** a capability missing for a class of customers. It goes to the product backlog with evidence.\n- **Capability limit:** not reliably achievable with current models. Reset expectations and propose\n  mitigations such as human review, narrower scope or confidence thresholds.\n\nAsk the classifying questions in a fixed order, cheapest and most urgent first. Does the system already\nattempt this? Look at the traces for the requester's example: if it tries and fails, it is a quality\nfailure, which goes first *because* users are being hurt now. If it succeeds when used correctly, it is a\nusage gap. Can existing configuration do it? Can current models do it reliably? Try it on a couple of\ndozen real examples before calling it a product gap, because \"the model can't\" and \"nobody tried\" look\nthe same from a ticket. Only then ask whether the need is unique to this customer or plausibly shared\nby others. Split compound asks: \"make pricing answers right and never wrong\" is one quality failure plus\none capability limit, and each half has a different route.\n\n### 3. Deduplicate and cluster across customers\nGroup requests by underlying problem, not by wording. One cluster with five accounts and concrete examples\nis far stronger evidence than five separate tickets. A shared tagging taxonomy across all field teams is\nwhat makes this possible.\n\n### 4. Score with explicit criteria\nA common framework is RICE, popularised by Intercom: Reach × Impact × Confidence ÷ Effort. Reach is\noriginally people or events per period; across customers, distinct accounts with the problem is a\nreasonable proxy. Impact uses a coarse scale (3 massive, 2 high, 1 medium, 0.5 low, 0.25 minimal),\nconfidence a percentage that discounts guesses, and effort person-weeks or person-months. The coarse\nscales are deliberate: they stop the team arguing over decimals and make the score a ranking aid, not a\nmeasurement. Add strategic fit, and risk (safety,\ncompliance, maintenance burden). Record commercial weight (ARR, deal-blocking status) as a *separate*\ninput, not multiplied into the score, *because* folding revenue into the score lets the biggest account set\nthe roadmap without anyone deciding that on purpose.\n\n### 5. Route and decide\nField fixes happen now. Product gaps go to the product owner, who decides; the FDE supplies evidence, not\ncommitments. Declines get a written reason and, where possible, a workaround. Apply a recurrence rule:\nwhen the same customer-specific extension has been built for several customers, propose productizing it.\n\n### 6. Close the loop\nTell every requester what happened and why, including \"no\". A visible outcome keeps customers reporting\nreal problems, instead of escalating the same ask to their executive sponsor.","example":"A weekly triage pass over seven captured requests from five accounts. Requests are clustered by underlying\nproblem and type, each cluster is routed, and only product gaps get a RICE score. ARR is shown next to the\nscore but kept out of it. Impact, confidence and effort are the team's estimates.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n# (account, arr_k, problem_key, kind)\nrequests = [\n    (\"Acme\", 400, \"cite-source-page\", \"product_gap\"),\n    (\"Globex\", 250, \"cite-source-page\", \"product_gap\"),\n    (\"Initech\", 120, \"cite-source-page\", \"product_gap\"),\n    (\"Acme\", 400, \"sap-form-export\", \"customer_specific\"),\n    (\"Globex\", 250, \"stale-fx-rates\", \"quality_failure\"),\n    (\"Umbrella\", 90, \"bulk-upload\", \"usage_gap\"),\n    (\"MegaCorp\", 2000, \"approval-workflow\", \"product_gap\"),\n]\nROUTE = {\n    \"quality_failure\": \"failure triage + regression case\",\n    \"usage_gap\": \"enablement; fix discoverability\",\n    \"customer_specific\": \"field build on extension point\",\n    \"product_gap\": \"product backlog with evidence\",\n}\n# Team estimates per product-gap cluster: impact (0.25-3), confidence (0-1), effort (weeks).\nESTIMATES = {\"cite-source-page\": (2, 0.8, 3), \"approval-workflow\": (2, 0.8, 3)}\n\nclusters = defaultdict(list)\nfor acct, arr, problem, kind in requests:\n    clusters[(problem, kind)].append((acct, arr))\n\nfor (problem, kind), rows in clusters.items():\n    accounts = {a for a, _ in rows}\n    arr = sum(dict(rows).values())          # ARR per distinct account\n    line = f\"{problem:17} {kind:17} accounts={len(accounts)} arr=${arr}k -> {ROUTE[kind]}\"\n    if kind == \"product_gap\":\n        impact, conf, effort = ESTIMATES[problem]\n        line += f\" | RICE={len(accounts) * impact * conf / effort:.2f}\"\n    print(line)\n```\n\nSeven tickets become five decisions. Page-level citations is one cluster spanning three accounts\n(RICE 3 × 2 × 0.8 ÷ 3 = 1.60, ARR $770k), so it goes to product with three concrete examples. The\napproval workflow is classed as a product gap because approval chains are common in the segment, but\nonly one account has asked; with identical estimates it scores RICE 0.53 at ARR $2,000k. Had reach been\nweighted by ARR, the order would flip: 2,000 against 770. Neither ordering is automatically right; the\nseparate column puts that choice in front of the product owner instead of letting arithmetic make it. The stale FX rates go straight into the quality loop this week.\nThe SAP export is built by the field team on the integration API. Bulk upload already exists, so the\naction is a training note and a UI change review.","tradeoffs":["Building a customer-specific feature in the field can save a deal now, but every fork taxes later upgrades; it is acceptable only on a supported extension point, with a recurrence rule for productizing it.","Weighting by ARR aligns with revenue but lets one large account dominate; keep it a separate, visible input so product leadership makes that trade-off deliberately.","Detailed capture and clustering take FDE time each week, but they turn anecdotes into evidence; for a single small pilot with one stakeholder, a lightweight list may be enough.","Sharing roadmap intent builds customer trust, but it creates implied commitments; share problem clusters and status, not delivery dates, unless product has committed."],"pitfalls":["Logging the proposed solution instead of the problem: the backlog fills with many near-duplicate items, and the cluster that matters never looks big.","Routing quality failures to the product backlog: users keep reporting the same wrong answers for weeks while the item waits for roadmap planning.","Field promises the product never made: the symptom is an escalation from the customer's executive asking where \"the committed feature\" is.","Letting the largest customer's asks dominate: the roadmap drifts toward one account's workflow, and other customers' adoption stalls.","Treating capability limits as features: an engineering effort chases \"never wrong\" for months instead of designing human review and scope limits.","No closure to requesters: the same asks reappear louder through other channels, and feedback from end users dries up."],"when":"Run triage whenever a deployed system has users who can ask for things, which is from the first pilot\nweek onward. Use a weekly cadence while volume is high, and hold a cross-customer review with product\nmonthly or per planning cycle. The full process matters most for platforms serving several customers.\nThere, clustering and the decline-with-reason discipline protect the roadmap. Measure time from request\nto decision, the share of requests reclassified as quality or usage gaps, and how many field extensions\nwere later productized. In interviews, the signal is separating problem from solution and routing\nAI-specific asks correctly.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the capture form and tagging, and turns quality-failure requests into regression cases instead of feature tickets.","architect":"Defines the supported extension points that make customer-specific asks buildable without forks, and sets the rule for when a pattern gets productized.","fde":"Interviews requesters to get to the underlying problem, classifies and clusters asks, and delivers clear decisions back to customers without making roadmap commitments."},"check":{"q":"A customer's VP says, \"We need the assistant to stop giving wrong answers about our pricing; put\nit on your roadmap for next quarter.\" How do you triage this?","a":"I would not treat it as a roadmap item. It is most likely a quality failure, so I collect concrete\nexamples and check the traces. Likely causes are retrieval of outdated pricing documents, missing\nsources, or a prompt that lets the model guess. Those go into failure triage now, with regression cases\nand a fix through the release gate, often within days. If part of the ask is \"never be wrong\", I explain\nthat as a model limit and propose mitigations: citing the pricing source, refusing when no document\nmatches, or human review for quotes. Then I tell the VP the plan and timeline."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should triage record the requester's underlying problem rather than their proposed solution?","options":["Proposed solutions are usually technically infeasible","Clustering across customers and choosing the best solution both depend on the problem, while solutions are worded differently","Product teams refuse to read solution proposals","It reduces the number of requests that must be logged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Different customers propose different solutions to the same problem. Capturing the problem reveals the real size of the need and leaves room for a better design than any single proposal."},{"q":"A user asks for the assistant to \"be more accurate on currency conversions\", and traces show it citing last year's FX table. Where should this go?","options":["The product backlog as a new feature for the next planning cycle","Enablement, because the user should check the rates themselves","The continuous-improvement loop as a quality failure, with a regression case and a data or retrieval fix","Decline it as a model capability limit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The system already tries to answer this and fails because of stale data. That is a quality failure, fixed fastest through failure triage and the release gate, not a roadmap item."},{"q":"Why keep ARR as a separate input rather than multiplying it into the priority score?","options":["ARR data is usually confidential and cannot be stored","Revenue should never influence product decisions","Folding it in lets the largest account set the roadmap implicitly, while a separate input makes that trade-off a deliberate decision","RICE scores are only defined for non-financial inputs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Revenue matters, but hidden inside a score it silently outweighs reach and impact. Showing it separately lets product leaders decide openly when to favour a big account."}]}]});
